window.harnessChunks["rows-26b24a609184799a.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-13T22:33:16+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KKKKhazix/agent-autoloop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent Autoloop</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-13\">2026-11-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KKKKhazix/agent-autoloop/tree/55189165943bcdd39b113faceb97b8e1eeacabbd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 55189165943bcdd39b113faceb97b8e1eeacabbd\">5518916 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Run goal-directed tasks in external authenticated CLI agent sessions bound by first-party rule and receipt protocol. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Scheduled work, source events, code/task novelty, tool feedback and paused human decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Running CLI agent chooses task solution, next action and structured receipt including ask, later, notice and new follow-up events. - Decision owner: Configured Codex/Claude/ACP/DSH CLI agent executing in supported first-party worker adapter; kernel only dispatches. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rules, Node worker, plugins, event log, durable session references and human card channel. - Closure path: Rule-matched event → Worker adapter invokes model-backed agent → result/receipt becomes kernel fact → follow-up event/ask/resume returns changed work to agent or its output. - Boundary reachability: Shipped worker and built-in adapters invoke installed agent processes; README documents real Claude/Codex/ACP multiround runs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: No model means deterministic scheduler and receipts cannot solve an open-ended task. - Evidence: worker/run.ts, worker/agents.ts, README.en.md, plugins/codex/agent.ts - Basis: structural and documented tested adapters -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Subject-scoped suppression of competing concurrently executing agent jobs routed into the same lane. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two asynchronous S1 workers may process duplicate events or mutate one subject concurrently. - Distinct S1 units: Independent worker invocations for queued runs, potentially multiple configured agents and rule groups. - Inter-S1 disturbance: A second run on the same (group,lane) could conflict with in-flight work; repeat event for queued subject could be redundant. - Attenuating coordination relation: Kernel enqueue consolidates queued same-rule/lane events; claimNext forbids claimed run with equal group and lane, respecting group/rule/agent capacity. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A claimed/finished run changes subsequent claim eligibility, allowing deferred worker invocation after the earlier owner releases lane. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Conflict witness is shared subject-lane exclusivity, not merely a global worker-count limit; the same-subject lock prevents concurrent S1 attempts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party group/lane coordination construction and worker lifecycle return are real, but agent-owned discretion over coordination is not shipped. - Decision owner: Deterministic kernel owns enforcement and developer composes agent task policy/subject granularity, so C rather than A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Event-sourced facts, queued/claimed run state, rules group, lane and maxActive. - Closure path: Source subject → enqueue/claimNext → exclusive lane reservation → worker finish/retry record → next queued run becomes eligible. - Boundary reachability: Run rules and claimNext execute in shipped kernel/host for configured worker adapters; unlike merely exposing a generic queue, lane exclusivity is function-specific. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the CLI models leaves materially same fixed conflict-avoidance policy; no agent selects coordination policy. - Evidence: kernel/decide.ts, kernel/rules.ts, README.en.md - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Human supervision of current hub-wide processing commitments and operating constraints. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Failed or blocked rounds, pending human decisions, unavailable agents, excess work and unreliable active rules. - Whole-system current view: CLI status/runs/why supplies current agent/rule workloads, pending cards, failures, usage and durations. - Current-control decision scope: Owner pauses/resumes particular rule, may replay skipped event scope, changes agent/rule concurrency and answers/halts in-flight decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Owner selects which present workloads run and at what rule-level capacity. - Decision owner: Legitimate local human owner of the hub config and CLI. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Status CLI, versioned rules, pause facts, claimNext gates, app notifications and cards. - Closure path: Current status/pending work → parent pause/answer/rule update → kernel facts and dispatch gate → subsequent running/queued work changes. - Boundary reachability: Shipped status, pause/resume, config hot reload and card-answer commands connect to active Node hub. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Configured CLI agents act inside their assigned tasks; no first-party model chooses hub-wide current operating constraints. - Evidence: README.en.md, kernel/decide.ts, host/runtime.ts, cli/explain.ts - Basis: structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Conduct a separate model-judged review of PR changes before optional merge/delivery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An authoring agent&#39;s report may conceal defects or unsafe changes. - Claim being audited: Changed PR code is sufficiently correct and safe for delivery under trusted main-branch policy. - Ordinary reporting path: Author PR description and status/CI. - Complementary access path: Review plugin captures actual read-only Git commit diff and review points from trusted main, not executing untrusted PR code. - Independence boundary: A dedicated ephemeral Codex/reviewer process reads isolated snapshot, with no checkout or code writes, separate from authoring CLI agent; review exemption is explicit policy. - Who acts on findings: Delivery flow returns findings to author for new commit/re-review or blocks merge until findings disposition is recorded; main gate governs merge. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reviewer model produces structured findings on direct diff evidence and its result controls subsequent delivery. - Decision owner: Configured independent reviewer agent instantiated by first-party delivery plugin. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Review ledger, signed head-specific status receipts, trusted review policy, scope/mode parser, full CI and delivery state machine. - Closure path: PR head → independent source snapshot and review → findings/ledger and GitHub status → remediate/refresh PR → review and merge gate. - Boundary reachability: Shipped delivery adapter and review.ts executes configured external CLI model in read-only isolated process when delivery rules are enabled. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A deterministic diff filter does not author discretionary findings; separate model reviewer does. - Evidence: plugins/delivery/review.ts, plugins/delivery/review-policy.ts, plugins/delivery/delivery.ts, README.en.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Optional delivery feature requires setup and can exempt reviewers under explicit policy. No live PR review replay in this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party prospectively generated option for changing the hub&#39;s own future organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Event feeds and history show changing environment but current tasks and future scheduled runs are not system adaptation. - External distinction: Incoming GitHub/webhook events, cron schedule and worker errors. - Future / prospective distinction: Scheduled later receipt addresses future task execution, not future organization design. - Adaptation option generated: No built-in adaptation hypothesis and capability-change decision traced. - Path back into current capability / S3: Operator can edit rules and plugins directly but not from a supported S4-specific prospective feedback loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Not present as S4. - Decision owner: None established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rules hot reload, events and periodic schedules. - Closure path: Current task outputs may schedule more work; this is S1 continuation, not S4 capability adaptation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A schedule and self-emitted task events do not establish autonomous future-environment option selection. - Evidence: kernel/decide.ts, kernel/rules.ts, README.en.md - Basis: structural negative -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party substantive identity/ultimate-policy decision-and-return path at the deployed hub recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Who handles events, agent capabilities and sensitive action approval are configured and enforced as task/operating rules. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: No described in-boundary mechanism for raising and deciding changes to the overarching system&#39;s charter and legitimate ultimate authority. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Human local administrator owns rules.json and installed agent credentials, but mere config edits do not establish S5 parent governance. - Return-to-operation path: Changed dispatch rules are enforced by hot reload, but this is not an evidenced policy-level proposal → legitimate deciding parent → returned charter decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separately constructed S5 right beyond external rule ownership. - Decision owner: No positive S5 mode established within reviewed first-party boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rules validation, local config, actor authorization, event data isolation and human cards. - Closure path: No evidenced identity-level decision loop; settings changes simply alter execution behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: CLI agent cannot redefine system purpose; operator changing a rule is not sufficient to infer an ultimate-policy governance arrangement. - Evidence: README.en.md, host/config.ts, kernel/rules.ts, protocol/rules.ts - Basis: structural negative -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3896a2de54399beb137f9e9959bb2587ebf4fc4a/assessments/ag/agent-autoloop.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3896a2de54399beb137f9e9959bb2587ebf4fc4a\">3896a2d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T21:29:46+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/BlackJaxDev/local-agent-broker\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Local Agent Broker</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/BlackJaxDev/local-agent-broker/tree/86b09380e3393126d6c540787a7576a61d21565b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 86b09380e3393126d6c540787a7576a61d21565b\">86b0938 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-controlled workers transform bounded objectives into answers, tool-mediated effects, or code-change proposals. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain objectives, changing tool results, failed or denied calls, and code-edit details in authorized source files. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The provider model selects its response, tool calls and next steps within broker-enforced limits; swarm leaves choose concrete source replacements. - Decision owner: External API model invoked as the active autonomous actor through the first-party broker loop; in swarm mode the per-phase root/leaf models choose plan and edits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MCP transport, exact-model check, prompt construction, tool registry, immutable snapshots, budgets, provider retries, session policy and parallelism semaphores. - Closure path: Caller starts a run → model chooses answer/tool action → broker executes allowed tool → result returns on the next model turn → completed answer; in swarm mode leaf proposals return through review and caller-controlled apply. - Boundary reachability: startagentrun invokes AgentRunRegistry.Start and AgentOrchestrator.RunAsync; optional swarm routes through RunSwarmAsync/RunNodeAsync in shipped production code, not a test-only agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model decides task content/actions. Hard tool caps and file permissions only constrain or reject those decisions, rather than replacing their discretion. - Evidence: MCP tool implementation, agent turn/tool feedback, swarm leaves. - Basis: Structural first-party model-execution and feedback path. - Confidence: High for code wiring; medium for real provider execution because no billed run was repeated here. - Caveats: A standalone broker invocation still needs explicit provider credentials and client authorization; provider cognition is not itself first-party broker implementation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No established adaptive inter-S1 conflict/oscillation attenuation with returned behavioral adjustment in the shipped modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel child agents can propose overlapping edits to a shared source file; separately queued workers can contend for concurrency capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The caller fixes allowed paths and limits; deterministic merger rejects overlapping source edits rather than negotiating a revised peer coordination response. - Decision owner: Caller for path allocations/limits; root model for initial task decomposition; runtime for conflict validation and static semaphore enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-child path assignment, immutable base hashes, overlap rejection, provider-slot and swarm-slot semaphores. - Closure path: Parallel child edits → conflict checker either combines non-overlapping patches or fails the proposal; no installed conflict-resolution decision goes back into a new coordinated child round. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Distinct S1 leaves and a real possible edit collision are present, but a rejection gate and initial delegation are not an S2-specific feedback regulator changing subsequent inter-S1 behavior. - Evidence: plan constraints, overlap detection, parallel execution. - Basis: Bounded negative functional assessment despite a structurally identifiable disturbance. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: External callers could retry with changed scopes; such a coordination actor/loop is not installed in the broker itself.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No autonomous whole-system current-control authority over a portfolio of active work and shared commitments beyond bounded delegation rules. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider failures, spending/turn/tool budgets, child-run admission, timeout and cancellation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Caller fixes model, task, tool permissions and budgets; swarm root generates a one-time file-scoped task plan, while the runtime enforces ceilings and aborts on failure. - Decision owner: Caller/operator owns shared limits and resource policy; model&#39;s root plan owns current task decomposition, not ongoing organizational capacity or commitments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentRunRegistry, global concurrency, AgentRunBudget, per-phase token reservation, node admission caps, failure propagation and status registry. - Closure path: Preset limits → runtime admission/scheduling/termination; the shipped loop lacks a whole-run current-state appraisal feeding discretionary reallocation of operational commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model task splitting is not evidenced whole-system current-resource bargaining or authoritative intervention; semaphores/budgets enforce caller choices. - Evidence: run registry, swarm plan, limits and failure propagation. - Basis: Bounded negative after testing root-orchestrator and runtime-manager paths. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Host clients may provide higher-recursion fleet S3; their decisions are outside this broker&#39;s boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Bounded model-owned source-backed review of child agent code-change claims before approval or application. - Claim being audited: Each leaf&#39;s proposed replacement is a valid contribution to the parent objective and success criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: Leaf model emits JSON path, basesha256, oldtext and newtext; runner collects child artifacts. - Complementary access path: A fresh parent review API request receives the unchanged child artifacts and broker-captured immutable source snapshots and original objective, not only the leaf&#39;s completion assertion. The deterministic merger separately validates base hashes/overlap. - Independence boundary: The review runs as a distinct provider phase after leaves finish, without their continuation or permission to modify their artifacts; the child cannot mark its own edits approved. The same exact model/provider family may be used and all inputs are broker-supplied snapshots, so this is bounded internal complementary inspection, not cross-model or external institutional audit. - Who acts on findings: RunNodeAsync rejects the entire swarm when the parent model returns approved=false; only positive review returns proposals to the run registry for caller review or explicit Windows application. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Plausible but faulty code replacements, mismatch to objective and misleading leaf success claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Separate parent reviewer model decides approved and summary after examining source and unchanged proposed edits; deterministic orchestrator enforces that verdict. - Decision owner: Review-phase provider model makes the substantive accept/reject judgment; runner parser and fail path are supporting enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Immutable source snapshot capture, isolated phase request with no tools, review-specific instructions, JSON parse, merge collision check and no-change-on-rejection guard. - Closure path: Leaf proposals → fresh model review with original source/artifacts → verdict → rejection terminates with no approved changes, or approval exposes the proposal for caller acceptance and possible readback-checked application. - Boundary reachability: Optional production swarm uses RunNodeAsync for every orchestrator node, creates a distinct CreatePhaseRequest with context-file snapshots for review, and RunSwarmAsync exposes only approved changes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer independently judges a source-backed child claim rather than trusting its self-report; the deterministic parser does not supply the substantive review decision. - Evidence: review request and input wiring, review verdict closure, provider snapshot input, rejection test. - Basis: Structural distinct audit-judgment call with source evidence and returned corrective veto. - Confidence: Medium: model decision and wiring established in source; actual provider-backed judgments not replayed. - Caveats: Same model/provider can produce correlated errors; review sees captured source and proposals, not independently sampled live post-apply code. This is not a general-purpose S3 of all broker runs.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped external/future environmental sensing, option formation, and adopted adaptation loop for the broker itself. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing provider options, future model costs/capabilities, repository evolution and new threats. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Caller selects actual model/configuration; keyword-based route advisor can recommend a model for the current objective, not revise future organizational capability. - Decision owner: Human caller/developer for model/policy upgrades; deterministic AgentRouteAdvisor for present-task recommendation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Exact-model catalog, task keyword classifier, provider error/retry diagnostics and operator settings. - Closure path: Current objective → static model recommendation → caller choice for this run; no external sensing→prospective options→capability adoption path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task difficulty routing and provider retries respond to current execution, not environmental foresight or adopted future change. - Evidence: route advisor, broker config, run/model validation. - Basis: Bounded negative after route/adaptation candidates were inspected. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: A user may delegate future-looking research to an individual worker; that is operational S1 output, not an established broker-level S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate identity/policy deliberation and legitimate return-to-runtime decision loop at this broker recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task authority, source-file permissions, credential exposure, destructive editor commands and uncontrolled billed model execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Caller and configuration supply boundaries; deterministic validators reject unauthorized requests. There is no shipped identity/charter question escalated to and resolved by an ultimate authority with a returned mandate. - Decision owner: External MCP caller/operator for task authorization and security policy; program validators enforce rather than govern its content. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Explicit workspace/allowed paths, tool annotations and allowlist, destructive/mutation flags, exact model requirement, budget checks and secret-path denial. - Closure path: Operator-supplied scope → fixed request guards → permitted/denied run; no identity-level issue→legitimate parent authority→binding policy decision→changed subsequent operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Ordinary permissions and operator review of a patch are run-level controls, not a first-party S5 organization or parent-governed identity loop. - Evidence: protocol policy, repository path policy, run admission. - Basis: Bounded negative for the standard-distribution authority path. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: The parent organization using the broker may have an S5; its external decisions are not part of this local harness&#39;s evidenced operating structure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a8b7d8aa1af85fabe5957742ed23c9358007621d/assessments/lo/local-agent-broker.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a8b7d8aa1af85fabe5957742ed23c9358007621d\">a8b7d8a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T17:29:23+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Open-RUA/uni-robot-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Uni Robot Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Open-RUA/uni-robot-agent/tree/2590ab48b7c24a72123597f2d5b04c9524e4329c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2590ab48b7c24a72123597f2d5b04c9524e4329c\">2590ab4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomous task execution through repeated robot observation, tool/action choice and resulting environmental feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing object poses, grasp failures, occluded views, tool errors, unavailable resources and differences between expected and measured effects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the configured coding-agent model chooses which available robot tool to call, with what arguments, whether to inspect/recover and when to request finish. - Decision owner: model-backed Codex/Claude task agent in the shipped run/TUI session; the SDK and provider implement its inference, while the first-party harness wires its decisions to robots. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party MCP/Claude tool adapters, shared Episode, resource executor, snapshots, action-size bounds, state-version checks, receipts, time/step budgets and structured feedback. - Closure path: task instruction and scene → model chooses a registered tool → executor/environment performs or rejects a bounded action → result, image and updated state return into the agent&#39;s next decision → task continues or finishes. - Boundary reachability: session.py actually selects and invokes a packaged Codex/Claude adapter with registered tools; no separate operator-authored control program is necessary after starting the supported model-backed episode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent has genuine contextual discretion among actions and recovery steps; deterministic guards and robot services restrict or carry out those choices but cannot replace the agent&#39;s task decisions. - Evidence: main run cycle, Claude SDK run and tool interaction, Codex SDK run, tool/episode flow. - Basis: structural code and explicit shipped run documentation; no live model-provider run was claimed in this review. - Confidence: high for the supported agent-to-tool/feedback decision path; physical task success remains unverified here. - Caveats: the model/SDK must be installed and configured; such dependencies do not make the first-party integrated agent path merely a non-agentic tool library.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct inter-S1 conflict-attenuation relation was established at the declared episode recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent motion/tool calls can conflict over an arm, an episode or its state version, but the reviewed paths serialize tools of one task-agent&#39;s execution rather than reconcile distinct autonomous S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: configured FIFO resource placement and immutable-snapshot rules govern tool execution; no first-party autonomous inter-S1 adjustment choice was found. - Decision owner: deterministic scheduler/developer configuration, not an S2 agent decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolExecutionPolicy, per-resource exclusive queues, background job handles, global-serial option and SDK subagent support. - Closure path: incompatible calls wait for a resource and receive completion feedback, but this is intra-episode execution safety, not a separately evidenced cross-S1 coordination conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: SDK-native subagents and two physical arms do not by themselves establish two organizational S1 units with their own outcomes and a specific reciprocal disturbance. - Evidence: scheduling and resource semantics, scheduler, agent adapter boundaries. - Basis: bounded negative inspection of shipped robot-tool and agent delegation modes. - Confidence: medium-high at the episode boundary. - Caveats: a separate multi-robot or multi-agent deployment might establish S2, but that composition is not inferred from SDK capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no independent whole-system current-control owner over multiple S1 commitments/resources was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: running time, token/tool limits, physical safety, stale observations and missed action acknowledgements are regulated locally within an episode. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator/developer selects budgets and modes; runtime rejects/queues/cancels actions according to those constraints, while the task agent chooses local work. - Decision owner: configuration/episode executor, not a distinct metasystem owner with current whole-system bargaining discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: timeout, step limit, resource queues, receipts, cancellation and TUI/CLI status. - Closure path: bounded local execution and failures feed the same agent&#39;s continuation; no portfolio-level current resource decision returns to several operational units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: calling the executor a controller does not establish S3; it does not independently allocate/revise commitments across an operational whole. - Evidence: session lifecycle, scheduler, architecture. - Basis: bounded absence review of implemented execution-control and user modes. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: parent human instructions and operational safety guards are real, but neither automatically closes S3 at this recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: an opt-in independently prompted model checks a subordinate VLA&#39;s proposed motor-action chunk against current raw observations, measured state and expected task progress, then can require a correction or stop before execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the Pi action model can propose an unsafe, geometrically misaligned or already-completed prefix despite producing a normal-looking trajectory. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reviewer model selects student, edit, eef or stop, number of steps, reason and continuation after judging the proposal and physical evidence; ActorRunner uses this decision to execute, modify or withhold the proposed chunk. - Decision owner: the separately invoked VLA reviewer model in supported RoboDojo Hybrid reviewer-enabled mode; deterministic validation/bounds constrain, but do not choose, its evaluative verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh camera capture, measured state and FK proposal, separate reviewer prompt/Responses call, structured validator, chunk archive, bounded correction commands and execution receipts. - Closure path: Pi proposes action prefix → independent reviewer receives proposal plus raw/derived observation → reviewer returns corrective or stop judgment → ActorRunner changes executed commands or halts → actual feedback and previous review enter the next chunk or higher-level task agent. - Boundary reachability: the shipped --action-profile hybrid path constructs ChunkReviewer by default when binding supports it, requires an explicit Responses-compatible endpoint, and calls its decide before any chunk execution. It is not a paper-only evaluator or an uninstalled CI process. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer can counter the subordinate actor&#39;s suggestion with independent discretionary evidence assessment and corrective action; it is not simply a deterministic success flag. This is mode-specific and cannot be generalized to Direct mode or to unconfigured endpoints. - Claim being audited: the proposed VLA action is aligned with current physical observations, task intent and local progress, and its continuation is warranted. - Ordinary reporting path: VLA proposal/trajectory and main task agent&#39;s instruction/task-progress report. - Complementary access path: separately prompted reviewer model sees raw head/wrist camera images, measured pose/FK checks, native feedback and prior reviewer context before command commitment. - Independence boundary: the VLA does not choose or edit the review verdict; the reviewer call has distinct prompt/context and can veto/correct, although the same provider/model family may be configured and both remain inside one product&#39;s action path. - Who acts on findings: ActorRunner applies stop, altered command prefixes or continued student actions; the main agent receives final tool feedback for its next choice. - Evidence: reviewer decision model, source-grounded reviewer prompt, execution after verdict, reviewer enablement. - Basis: structural first-party review/judgment and actuation-return wiring; no separate provider E2E was performed. - Confidence: medium for materially complementary access, high for the executable verdict-to-command decision path. - Caveats: no assurance of model reliability or institutionally independent review follows. Hybrid requires a model endpoint, action checkpoint and integration. This state credits the installed optional reviewer path, not routine task success checks or offline evaluation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated first-party outside-and-prospective option-selection conversation returning to current whole-episode capability was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future environments, robot geometries and repeated failures could motivate improved skills or action models. - Decisive decision or feedback right: writeskill optionally lets the task agent store an observed lesson in an operator-configured external library; developers choose model/integration updates. Neither shows a separate established future-environment intelligence and S3 adaptation loop. - Decision owner: task agent owns local lesson text when enabled; operator/developer chooses whether/how to reuse broader configuration and model improvements. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: opt-in skill-writing, read/list skill tools, prompt guidance that requires evidence and narrow transfer, saved traces and optional SFT export. - Closure path: a verified task lesson may be stored for a later run, but review found no first-party prospective external-option decision with a defined whole-system capability adoption/return path; ordinary memory reuse is not sufficient. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: experience preservation can alter later task context, but it is not by itself S4&#39;s external-and-then intelligence conversation. - Evidence: skill-writing implementation, lesson instructions, architecture&#39;s optional skill mode. - Basis: bounded negative review distinguishing operational experience from prospective environmental adaptation. - Confidence: medium; externally oriented future modes may be added later. - Caveats: this does not deny useful cross-run skill improvement or possible future S4 constructor work.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate runtime identity/ultimate-policy issue-to-authority-to-operation loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicts over robot-task safety, data/model choice, user purpose and deployment policy could raise higher-level identity questions, but these remain outside the reviewed episode governance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a user chooses task/mode/constraints; first-party runtime enforces tool permissions and safety bounds, without a distinct S5 deliberation/return path. - Decision owner: external human/project authority for product identity; task-local model/guard machinery does not own ultimate policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLI configuration, model/tool permissions, tool restrictions, explicit finish acknowledgement and environment safety state. - Closure path: configuration constrains next actions and safety checks reject disallowed movement; no observed identity dispute is escalated to a legitimate parent and returned as a revised binding ultimate policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: human task instructions and deterministic safety policy do not become autonomous or parent-governed S5 merely because they are authoritative for one episode. - Evidence: configuration, run structure, episode and tools. - Basis: bounded negative inspection of runtime control, human interaction and governance scope. - Confidence: medium-high at the declared episode boundary. - Caveats: the external robotics organization may possess its own S5; it is not part of this first-party deployed harness by repository adjacency.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b1c0dda8f43c21cc9cbab1d1db5c651a537454d4/assessments/un/uni-robot-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b1c0dda8f43c21cc9cbab1d1db5c651a537454d4\">b1c0dda &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T20:07:24+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/phuryn/mini-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mini Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/phuryn/mini-harness/tree/7ae9fca8221c55bd3c38697a61075357e1a94c26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7ae9fca8221c55bd3c38697a61075357e1a94c26\">7ae9fca &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-guided local work with iterative tool-result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing files, shell command outputs, tool errors, user objectives and MCP responses. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model chooses next tool and arguments after observations, can stop or delegate a bounded reading task. - Decision owner: Model executing in first-party Agent.turn/dispatch; installed harness executes selected calls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LocalTools, approvals, MCP hub, append-only session, context compaction and goal mode. - Closure path: User/goal → model inference → selected local/MCP tool → real result → appended observation → another model choice or answer. - Boundary reachability: Supported CLI instantiates an actual model client and supplies tools; fake-model tests exercise the same loop, but paid-provider operation was not replayed here. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model is able to choose an action and change it from observations; tool runtime and permissions do not own the choice. - Evidence: loop and dispatch, tools, session. - Basis: First-party model→tool→observation→redecision implementation and reachable CLI. - Confidence: High for code path; no paid-provider E2E claim. - Caveats: Provider behavior, external MCP cognition and human permission remain outside first-party ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No ongoing regulation of contention between multiple autonomous operational S1 units is installed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel demands or access conflict among distinct workers could arise, but task delegates a standalone reading request rather than balancing competing S1 commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Main agent may delegate; no independent conflict arbitration loop decides inter-worker priorities. - Decision owner: Main S1 model for delegation and operator for permission; none for a separate S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Fresh-session task subagents, tool approval and session separation. - Closure path: Main model→child task→child text→main model; no discovered contention→S2 arbitration→corrected peer commitments path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Sequential or concurrent delegation is not itself feedback regulation between S1 units. - Evidence: subagent invocation, session. - Basis: Bounded absence review of installed delegate and tool gates. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: User-built external MCP servers might coordinate their own agents outside this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No discretionary control of present resources, priorities and commitments across a whole set of autonomous S1 operating units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Goal turn/budget exhaustion, failed verification, tool risk and paused work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: /goal performs fixed turn/budget/status gates and deterministic pass-or-retry; users choose the verification command and budget. - Decision owner: Human operator owns scope/budgets; code enforces fixed conditions. Main model still owns task steps. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: rungoal turn limit, spend meter, verify, permission approval and pause/resume. - Closure path: Claimed completion→verification/reviewer gate→accept or return disagreement to same S1; no discretionary cross-unit resources/commitment management. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Strong operational gating does not itself imply a separate S3 decision authority over an S1 portfolio. - Evidence: goal and verification, permission gate. - Basis: Bounded current-control absence despite a real goal-acceptance gate. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A separately deployed fleet supervisor could add S3, but is not a first-party standard mode here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Separate fresh-context inspection of a worker&#39;s completion claim using additional operational evidence before accepting a goal. - Claim being audited: The main model declares that the current /goal objective is fully complete and supplies goaldone evidence. - Ordinary reporting path: The worker&#39;s tool observations and self-reported completion argument arrive through its existing session and goal claim. - Complementary access path: Fresh-context reviewer receives the objective and claim and can directly inspect current working-directory files using its own tool calls; optional shell verifier independently executes user-specified checks. - Independence boundary: Distinct reviewer session and tool calls but the same first-party code/provider client and host permission policy; reviewer is not a separately governed institution. - Who acts on findings: Agent.rungoal rejects unapproved completions, returns negative reviewer findings to worker and repeats until accepted or stopped. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False self-reported completion, missing/incorrect local artifacts and a worker&#39;s biased summary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reviewer independently reads working-directory evidence and returns VERDICT: DONE or rejection; goal controller accepts only a positive verdict after optional independent shell verification. - Decision owner: Fresh reviewer model for the audit judgment; harness for deterministic acceptance and rework routing; operator for optional verify definition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: New reviewer session, actual LocalTools read/bash access, distinct review instructions, optional shell verify, literal verdict parser and rework prompt. - Closure path: Worker goaldone claim→independent shell check when configured→new reviewer reads workspace→review judgment→goal controller acceptance or discrepancy returned to worker→corrective next turn. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reviewer receives the goal and claim but has a genuinely separate workspace observation path and can reject the claim, rather than merely grading its prose; the gate only enforces the decision. - Evidence: REVIEW, runsubagent, rungoal, verify, reviewer false-claim test. - Boundary reachability: Supported /goal invokes verify on goaldone; bundled tests cover rejection, retry and fresh reviewer tools, without requiring a hypothetical external plugin. - Basis: Source-traced complementary evidence access, separate judgment, explicit corrective return in supported /goal mode. - Confidence: Medium-high for installed bounded path, not independent external audit quality. - Caveats: Same provider/client and workspace permissions; reviewer may be wrong, can be influenced by claim text, and is triggered by worker&#39;s completion claim. Independence is complementary to the worker&#39;s ordinary report, not organizational or institutional independence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No autonomous prospective environment-scanning and strategy/adaptation cycle for the harness organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model availability, new skills and MCP sources, changing user needs and context size. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human configures external services and models; compaction summarizes current conversation and goal remains a current task. - Decision owner: Operator for future capability selection, present S1 for ordinary research within user request. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Skills, MCP discovery, model presets, compaction, session resume and subagent research. - Closure path: Updated config or task findings may change next operation; no future-environment forecast→strategic selection→adopted change in S3 operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Compaction/memory and delegated investigation support present tasks without autonomous mandate to reconfigure future capacity. - Evidence: loop/model config, external service hooks. - Basis: Bounded absence review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Individual agent may discuss future scenarios as an S1 user task.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No distinct ultimate identity-policy or parent-governed mandate cycle in the installed task harness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Permission-sensitive requests, unsafe tool invocations and operator goal changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator approves/denies risky actions and sets goal; optional cheap-model permission judge recommends local allowances under a fixed gate. - Decision owner: External user and static permission rules; no independent legitimate S5 authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Approval prompts, optional permission judge, goal entry and local policy/configuration. - Closure path: User/permission decision→tool allow/deny; no identity-level conflict→legitimate policy decision→returned organizational mandate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Permission vetting is a task action constraint, not constitutive identity governance. - Evidence: permissions, goal setup. - Basis: Bounded absence review, including model-assisted permission path. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: External organization or human can exercise S5 outside this first-party product.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f4373c2a406cda6a71e08c5c68ac4810c7d980da/assessments/ph/phuryn-mini-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f4373c2a406cda6a71e08c5c68ac4810c7d980da\">f4373c2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T21:20:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/genproof/QuickMaths\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">QuickMaths</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/genproof/QuickMaths/tree/c2dbb59e13b3cce451b86e600345361b34e8c5ba\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c2dbb59e13b3cce451b86e600345361b34e8c5ba\">c2dbb59 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Transform a mathematical problem or contracted proof lane into proof artifacts through successive agent choices and compiler observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open proof holes, library availability, compiler errors, failed tactics, quota limits and continued work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose mathematical steps, edits, searches and next proof tactics within the supplied problem/lane contract. - Decision owner: Model-driven proving agent in the concrete first-party prepared job/role composition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pinned external Claude Code executes model rounds; first-party agentrunner, Landlock boundary, ./lean broker, snapshots, role briefs, budgets and continuation sustain them. - Closure path: Prepared job/brief launches the pinned CLI with workspace tools; ./lean returns build/search/inspect observations; later edits/rounds consume those observations and retained notes/artifacts. Official verification alone controls final acceptance. - Boundary reachability: controller prepares and starts the packaged runtime; feeder attaches actual role briefs to assignments. This credits the shipped proving composition, without borrowing unrelated Claude Code functions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model-driven prover leaves supervision and compilation but no choice of proof construction or repair. - Evidence: fleet/server/controller.py; runtime/leaneval/jobs.py; runtime/leaneval/agentrunner.py; runtime/leaneval/worker.py; fleet/server/briefs/COMMON.md - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Regulate conflicting interfaces and duplicate shared machinery among concurrent proof-lane S1 cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A consumer needs a stronger producer statement, a lane refutes a shared assumption, or consumers independently re-derive incompatible shared machinery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Architect grants/refuses interface requests, revises producing/consuming contracts and preserves only still-valid finished lanes. - Decision owner: The model-driven ARCHITECT/REARCHITECT agent at the lane-program coordination boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Lane write confinement, InterfaceRequests/refutation extraction, validated reductions/sanity statements, bounded rearchitecture, merged overlays and redispatch enforce and transport the chosen response. Fleet de-duplication is an additional deterministic path, not the agent owner. - Closure path: LANE writes a concrete request/refutation; feeder collects it and supplies findings to a later architect; architect chooses corrected statements; validated architecture becomes the new base and subsequent lane assignments use those contracts. - Boundary reachability: feeder updateprogram/programassignment wire the shipped role briefs and findings into actual model rounds, then replace active lane state after validation. This is a supported operating path, not contributor dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic code can detect/reforward a mismatch, but cannot choose the corrected mathematical interface or grant/refuse the request without the architect. - Distinct S1 units: Concurrent producer and consumer LANE proof work cells, each with a separate proof outcome and directory. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Consumer requirements exceed producer contracts or a refuted interface invalidates dependent lanes; concrete request/refutation fields and source tests expose this collision. - Attenuating coordination relation: Shared interface contracts, one shared-tool producer, request-to-architect revision and invalidation of dependent completed claims. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Corrected architecture replaces lane statements and subsequent dispatched/restarted work uses the new base; invalid consumers cannot remain done. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The return changes a specific producer–consumer interface conflict rather than merely assigning tasks or transporting results. - Evidence: fleet/server/briefs/roles/ARCHITECT.md; fleet/server/briefs/roles/LANE.md; fleet/server/briefs/roles/REARCHITECT.md; fleet/server/feeder.py; fleet/tests/testfeeder.py - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: Regulate current campaign commitments, placement, continuation, competing routes and scarce account/runner resources. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Quota-exhausted work alongside idle usable accounts, duplicated programs, stalled lineages and unsuitable placements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select/revise fleet policies and current interventions: hold/release, stop, hand off, split, hedge or rearchitect on behalf of the campaign. - Decision owner: Base C: a developer must compose the discretionary autonomous campaign controller over the specific current-view/plan/inbox/operator interfaces. Parent P: the legitimate campaign experiment manager/operator owns those choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Deterministic coordinator.plan/execute, feeder scheduling, inbox outcomes, locks, controlled STOP and quota gates enforce selected constraints; their fixed rules are not autonomous organizational judgment. - Closure path: Gathered fleet status and lane/claim evidence inform manager decisions; --apply/inbox and operator commands change holds, claims, seeds or program architecture; feeder readback and later assignments enact the decision. - Boundary reachability: Deployment docs explicitly expose fleet status, coordinator apply and leh op to the experiment manager. Unlike a generic kill switch, these are campaign-specific current-control interfaces. No automatically running agent manager is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: With the manager removed, configured rules still enforce preselected policy but do not independently revise campaign priorities. A possible supervised agent session does not establish autonomous ownership of the reserved manager decisions. - Whole-system current view: Coordinator gathers hosts, accounts, live lineages, passes, targets, programs and prior directive outcomes; manager reads campaign status plus residual/quota information. - Current-control decision scope: Cross-host resource/commitment intervention and campaign/program continuation/route decisions, rather than just one proof tool call. - Evidence: fleet/local/coordinator.py; fleet/server/feeder.py; fleet/docs/COORDINATOR-POLICY.md; fleet/operator/README.md; docs/EXPERIMENTMANAGER.md - Basis: structural with explicit documented parent procedure - Confidence: high for P and specific construction surfaces; no A claim - Caveats: C does not grade deterministic scheduling as agent-owned. Parent authority is local to the operated campaign, not the OSS contributor organization.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Complement ordinary progress claims with direct proof-graph/statement inspection of a nonconverging program. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Self-reported RESIDUALS estimates can shrink through renaming while actual holes remain; false/vacuous lane assumptions can invalidate apparent progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge mismatch between agent progress claims and direct Lean declaration/statement reality, then require corrective intervention. - Decision owner: A separately composed experiment-manager auditor; the first-party audit-specific procedure and evidence/control path are supplied, but autonomous auditor execution/authority is not automatically wired. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: leh map builds the merged tree and extracts actual reached/sorry-dependent declarations/refutations; operator tools expose artifacts/notes and rearchitect/stop updates. Lean execution and JSON summaries are evidence machinery, not the audit-judgment owner. - Closure path: METHOD §9 requires periodic map-versus-RESIDUALS comparison; the manager stop-and-audit procedure inspects statement truth/source strength and routes findings into rearchitecture/hedges or corrected program briefs through leh op. The direct map is advisory until that actor acts. - Boundary reachability: The installed leh map and deployed operator tools are linked by the supported manager method. It is an intentionally exposed campaign-audit construction, distinct from repository AUDIT.md or mandatory official acceptance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The map exposes materially different facts, but does not itself decide or implement the audit judgment; the supervised manager/agent setup remains to be composed for autonomous closure. - Claim being audited: Reported progress/completion and validity/usefulness of lane statements. - Ordinary reporting path: NOTES RESIDUALS/HANDOFF, lane done state and feeder convergence summaries. - Complementary access path: Build the merged proof tree, walk actual Lean declaration reachability/sorry/refutation dependencies, inspect published proof and concrete statement counterexamples. - Independence boundary: Manager does not prove the lanes; direct graph/statement access is outside self-reported estimates. Official verifier alone is not treated as S3. - Who acts on findings: Experiment manager selects corrective stop/rearchitecture/hedge; operator tools return findings to architect and subsequent lane rounds. - Evidence: docs/METHOD.md; docs/EXPERIMENTMANAGER.md; src/harness/map/build.py; src/harness/map/summary.py; fleet/operator/rearchitect.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: No autonomous always-on auditor is claimed, and this assessment did not run Lean graph extraction. Human-owned audit is not published as P in this Methodology.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Develop the campaign capability repertoire from external mathematical/library opportunities under experiment-manager direction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Upcoming proof ingredients absent from current workbench versus existing licensed formalizations, changed/problematic benchmark statements and future evaluation feasibility. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose external reusable sources or revised methods to bring into the proving capability, with human acceptance and rollout. - Decision owner: The legitimate campaign experiment manager; documentation explicitly says a person decides/applies adaptation. A supervised operator agent can assist without establishing autonomous adaptation authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Manager literature/web recon and evolve reports support option development; bank add pins/license-checks sources, deploy distributes accepted briefs/policies, and worker search exposes admitted bank entries. - Closure path: Manager compares current convergence/capability gaps with published sources and future campaign needs, chooses reuse/admission or method change, adds pinned formalizations to the durable workbench bank or commits/redeploys approved changes; later architects/lanes search/use them and report reuse. Subsequent reports compare effects. - Boundary reachability: METHOD §§6,8,10 and manager instructions explicitly connect external recon to the bank/briefs; bank.py and worker.py implement the returned capability change. Evolve alone, being retrospective internal analysis, would not establish S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model proof search and deterministic proposal reports cannot independently approve/admit campaign adaptation; the documented manager remains the decisive parent. - External distinction: Relevant published mathematics/formalizations, benchmark issue information and organizer resource envelope beyond the current proof workspace. - Future / prospective distinction: Whether forthcoming campaign/lane work should reuse new ingredients or change method rather than repeat unsupported construction; durable bank admission changes the repertoire of later rounds. - Adaptation option generated: Acquire a licensed pinned formalization as a reusable tool source, revise role briefs/gates, or retain the current capability after comparison. - Path back into current capability / S3: Manager bank admission makes new sources searchable by the running runtime; accepted policy/brief changes are tested/deployed and used by later fleet assignments. - Evidence: docs/METHOD.md; docs/EXPERIMENTMANAGER.md; docs/EVOLVE.md; runtime/tools/bank.py; runtime/leaneval/worker.py; fleet/server/briefs/roles/ARCHITECT.md - Basis: explicit documented operating procedure plus structural return path - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: A search for a present proof ingredient alone is S1; credit is limited to the documented manager capability-acquisition/rollout loop. No campaign transcript, measured adaptation benefit or autonomous Director is claimed.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Standing safety/source/acceptance rules and a person deciding submission/publication constrain the campaign; no runtime identity/ultimate-policy issue process with legitimate authoritative return is supplied. Experiment-manager current control and approved method updates do not automatically become S5. - Disturbance / variety regulated: No distinct function-specific regulation established at this boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None supplied for this organizational function. - Decision owner: No first-party function owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Static source/credential/acceptance constraints, profile.toml licensing rules, reviewed evolution commits and publish/trust decisions; none establishes a distinct identity-policy deliberation/escalation loop. - Closure path: No function-specific returned regulation; the operational mechanisms remain as described. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewed mechanisms do not establish the function before ownership classification. - Evidence: docs/EXPERIMENTMANAGER.md; docs/EVOLVE.md; docs/CONFIGURATION.md; runtime/leaneval/resources/profile.toml; fleet/local/publish.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/dc4fff62cbaa1faa7b2e604db7a71ece9e95521b/assessments/qu/quickmaths.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit dc4fff62cbaa1faa7b2e604db7a71ece9e95521b\">dc4fff6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T13:50:49+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/beipo-ai/NorthCUA\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NorthCUA</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/beipo-ai/NorthCUA/tree/fb5f9e2725c7cfe268fa0c2dc40f44cc132c12f4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fb5f9e2725c7cfe268fa0c2dc40f44cc132c12f4\">fb5f9e2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out one user computer-use task through observed Desktop/Web state and model-chosen actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown application screens, changed DOM/state, unexpected tool responses, context growth, unsatisfied task conditions and execution interruptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose next computer-use/tool action or finish based on observed state and returned tool results. - Decision owner: Configured agent model operating in the first-party Desktop/Web round executor; external inference service supplies model computation but does not implement the NorthCUA task loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: NexAU-compatible model adapter, typed tool contracts, context/image window, working notes, action backend, controller/device bridge, step budgets and persisted history. - Closure path: User request → contextual observation → model step/action choice → constrained tool execution → new screenshot/DOM/tool result and notes → subsequent model step or terminal result. - Boundary reachability: The documented local Web/Desktop CLI and remote task modes instantiate this actor loop using bundled AgentKit specs and first-party tool dispatch; no test-only harness or adjacent research agent is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model selects substantive environment actions after seeing observations. Tool schemas, transport, budget and safety gates constrain or carry this discretion rather than select it. - Evidence: Web agent specification, Desktop step executor, runtime architecture. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the first-party model/tool loop; no live hosted-provider success claim. - Caveats: S1 depends on configured model/device/backend availability, and the assessment does not measure task efficacy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate interference between distinct simultaneously admitted batch task attempts, especially duplicate execution, resource oversubscription and accidental state reuse. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent attempts may claim the same task, worker/device capacity or prepared environment after restarts, timeouts and parallel preparation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select or revise which attempt can claim a lease and when backpressured capacity may admit subsequent tasks; a higher-level autonomous coordination policy would have to be composed to own the discretion. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic scheduler, leases and persisted rules select immediate admission based on human/developer-provided limits; there is no demonstrated autonomous agent choosing inter-task coordination policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SQLite task claim/heartbeat, attempt identity, task-scoped sandbox/device lease, backpressure samples, single-use prepared reservation and reconciliation. - Closure path: Contending batch attempts → exclusive task/lease claim and controller-pressure check → one attempt proceeds, conflicting contenders wait or reconcile → subsequent tasks claim only released/eligible capacity. - Boundary reachability: Batch mode ships these scheduler/lease components and documented resume semantics; unlike a hypothetical generic queue, the code specifically handles simultaneous task claims and attempt reuse. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Concrete inter-S1 conflict attenuation exists, but its decisive runtime rule is deterministic; a first-party S2-specific constructor path is exposed without a shipped model-owned policy adjustment loop. - Distinct S1 units: Independently running Desktop/Web task attempts with their own task goals, environments and actor loops under a single batch. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Duplicate task submissions or competing sandbox/device admissions and reused application state when attempts overlap or a runner resumes. - Attenuating coordination relation: Unique task/attempt claims and task-scoped leases, bounded concurrency, backpressure gating and selected retry/reconcile rules. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Denied/late claims do not start duplicate actor operations; capacity availability and persisted task status determine later submission and resume. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The coordination enforces a named cross-attempt collision and resource contention invariant and feeds a concrete admission/withholding result into subsequent distinct task attempts. - Evidence: batch scheduler, lease coordinator, backpressure gate, batch recovery documentation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for an actual inter-attempt coordination path, medium for constructor classification. - Caveats: The batch is optional; an individual local run has no distinct S2 peer set. Deterministic conflict avoidance cannot be upgraded to S2=A without autonomous coordination discretion.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Whole-batch current commitments and running capacity can be supervised by a legitimate operator who controls admission, pause, cancellation, drain and selected retries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Failed or uncertain attempts, exhausted retry budgets, workload pressure, controller interruptions and cleanup problems across a batch. - Decisive decision or feedback right: View current aggregate task statuses and choose to stop new admissions, cancel current work, drain running tasks, resume or authorize specific failed-task retries. - Decision owner: The first-party mode&#39;s human batch operator. The scheduler and SQLite state apply that returned decision; no independent AI whole-batch manager is shipped. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Status/report CLI, persisted control flags and task states, pause/drain/cancel handlers, selected-retry plan validation and scheduler poller. - Closure path: Operator reviews whole-batch status/report → invokes pause/drain/cancel or bounded selected retry/resume → persistent flags/attempt ceilings are read by the live scheduler → current/new task admissions and execution change. - Boundary reachability: The documented northcua batch status|report|pause|drain|cancel|resume commands and scheduler&#39;s control-flag poller form a usable first-party operator mode over the same persisted batch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This is an operationally returned parent mode (P), not automatic agent S3; deterministic capacity thresholds and external operator actions do not supply autonomous S3=A or C by themselves. - Whole-system current view: Persisted all-task states and report/failure breakdown across one identified batch, including attempts and evaluation statuses. - Current-control decision scope: Stop or resume new admissions, cancel active work, drain committed tasks and select permitted retries for failed tasks, affecting whole-batch present commitments. - Evidence: batch operator commands, CLI branch handling, control-flag scheduler, selected retry plan. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; owner/control return is directly coded, but no actual operator run was reproduced in this assessment. - Caveats: Parent governance is only at a single batch&#39;s current-operations recursion, not at the OSS project&#39;s policy/identity recursion. This finding does not classify arbitrary status dashboards as S3.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: A separate evaluator agent challenges actor completion against independently gathered artifact and optionally live application reality. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agent self-report may disagree with task requirements, unsaved application state, missing artifacts or unseen outcome failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select relevant raw/recorded evidence and optional live probes, judge requirements and submit a structured success/failure report distinct from actor termination. - Decision owner: The separately instantiated first-party model-driven evaluator, through its own tool-selection and final-report loop; not the actor, trace writer or deterministic batch status projector. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Evidence manifest, read-only artifact and live probe tools, bounded evaluator step execution, report schema, fail-open/fail-closed configuration and batch result projection. - Closure path: Actor completion → independent artifact/live evidence collection → evaluator model verdict in finalreport → recorded outcome distinct from actor claim → batch task becomes success/failure/pending and controls downstream retry/reporting decisions. - Boundary reachability: Shipped local/remote Web/Desktop evaluator modes can be enabled in app configuration or CLI, and the Web evaluator runs before releasing the actor&#39;s original browser session. A direct live probe is conditional on explicit permission; the artifact-evidence route remains independently wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The evaluator chooses direct evidence inspection and a task-grounded judgment in its own model/tool loop; its report changes terminal status. Deterministic tooling does not make that independent audit judgment. - Claim being audited: Whether the actor actually satisfied the user&#39;s task despite its own reported completion. - Ordinary reporting path: Actor terminal result, its model/tool traces, screenshots and action outputs. - Complementary access path: Separate evaluator&#39;s evidence selection and tool access to saved artifacts plus, when enabled, fresh DOM/screenshot or allowed device read-only probes against the original live session. - Independence boundary: The evaluator executes in a separate model-round/runtime and has evidence/probe tools unavailable to ordinary self-report; it is still a first-party evaluation path, not an externally independent scientific audit or proof of blind reviewer independence. - Who acts on findings: Batch status projection treats a present negative verdict as failure, while operator/retry flow may admit a subsequent attempt; no claim that the same actor is automatically corrected before finalization. - Evidence: evaluator loop, Web evaluator service, read-only probe contracts, terminal projection, evaluation modes. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for independently wired model audit and returned verdict, not for real-world judge accuracy. - Caveats: Evaluation may be disabled or fail open; live probes require permission and original session availability; a status verdict affects subsequent batch handling rather than guaranteeing a corrective rerun. No actual hosted model, external evaluator or empirical accuracy study was executed for this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped S4-specific prospective external-environment model and adaptation-option decision returning to present organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changes in apps/sites, task complexity and model quality are seen by current actor/evaluator, but not separately explored as future environment adaptation at the batch/harness recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Generate future-facing capability/policy adaptation options from external distinctions and negotiate them with current operational control. - Decision owner: Project developers/operators may separately plan upgrades; actor plans current tasks, evaluator judges completed work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Working notes, image/context compaction, feedback artifacts, model/provider configuration, benchmark and research documentation. - Closure path: Task observations and evaluator reports feed current run/batch status, not a first-party outside-and-then option/implementation return loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task planning, context memory, runtime retries and post-run quality reporting are not themselves prospective adaptation judgments. - Evidence: system tour, context mechanisms, batch lifecycle. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high within the reviewed distribution. - Caveats: Research and model upgrades can support future work outside this runtime boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No system-level identity or ultimate-policy resolution channel with a legitimate deciding owner and returned binding decision is shipped for the assessed task/batch organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Risky computer actions, provider/device access, evaluation-probe mutability and cost/time limits are bounded by operator-set configuration and tool controls. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Adjudicate contested identity/purpose/ultimate-policy matters and return binding changed policy to the running organization. - Decision owner: Users/developers choose static configuration and external provider access; no first-party S5 decision actor or parent appeal path is established for such matters. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only evaluator probes by default, explicit mutating-tool opt-in, sandbox capabilities, model/task budgets, run authorization and cancellation. - Closure path: Current operator configuration → enforcement of permitted tools/budgets, with no identity-level proposal → parent authority → decision → changed operation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Ordinary permission and task cancellation choices are not ultimate-policy decisions, and no shipped independent policy/identity deliberation return is identified. - Evidence: evaluation configuration, batch configuration, architecture. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high at assessed operation/batch boundary. - Caveats: Project maintainers naturally retain licensing/release governance, but that adjacent project-level authority cannot be silently credited as runtime S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0ad99b51aef44dd4a35185e7ecf73717a6d29557/assessments/no/northcua.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0ad99b51aef44dd4a35185e7ecf73717a6d29557\">0ad99b5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T20:13:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/halayhq/halay\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">halay</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/halayhq/halay/tree/49bccfcb75e805b7ab55bb40c92a109dcdc62c24\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 49bccfcb75e805b7ab55bb40c92a109dcdc62c24\">49bccfc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Distinct user-task agents operate in their assigned project directories through the launched configured CLI. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing user requests, project state and subprocess/tool feedback delivered to each CLI session. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Each running model-backed CLI agent chooses task actions and next turns, while halay controls the first-party spawning, routing, replies and child communication interface. - Decision owner: Individual CLI-backed model task actor, through halay&#39;s installed multi-agent composition; not Node HTTP request routing alone. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Backends, per-agent cwd/account/model, MCP swarm tools, event stream, permissions and optional worktrees. - Closure path: User/conductor task→halay launches addressed CLI agent→upstream engine acts in workspace→result/status returns via halay→model continues, receives follow-up or returns result. - Boundary reachability: src/server.js launches supported CLI processes and src/swarm-mcp.js exposes actionable tools; this requires one external CLI installed and authenticated, and does not credit its underlying internal tool loop as a first-party implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Real CLI-backed models have discretion over work; the first-party control plane enables and observes their running roles. The launcher alone would not be autonomous. - Evidence: spawn and task lifecycle, backends, swarm tools. - Basis: Supported executable agent-CLI composition and returned operational state, not claimed first-party model internals. - Confidence: Medium-high for first-party runtime integration, not upstream vendor correctness. - Caveats: The prerequisite external CLI supplies cognition, local tool semantics and vendor behavior; absent a configured backend, halay is an idle control plane.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separately demonstrated autonomous attenuation of an observed conflict among the multiple operating child units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Shared-workspace collisions and competing task dependencies are plausible; the default task DAG orders work but is authored and assigned as a workflow, not measured inter-S1 contention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party dependency gate rejects blocked starts; conductor can manually/model-select routing as S3 rather than independent S2 balancing. - Decision owner: User/conductor for assignments, deterministic task dependency guard for eligibility. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task graph dependson, worktrees, agent message bus, parent-child scoped tools and queues. - Closure path: Task dependency→ready/waiting/blocked gate or manual assignment; messages travel between actors, but no installed contention sensing→S2 arbitration→revised peer behavior loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool messages, worktree isolation and static dependency checks are valuable coordination infrastructure without evidence of a specific separately owned S2 regulator. - Evidence: task dependency/agent management, swarm tool interface. - Basis: Bounded absence after testing strongest task-DAG and peer-messaging candidates. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A conductor can respond to a user-observed conflict, but no distinct routine S2 feedback mandate was found in the shipped setup.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Shipped model-driven conductor supervises the present agent fleet and allocates/revises task commitments and capacity. - Whole-system current view: Conductor&#39;s listagents, task listing, status/attention, child-result notifications and account availability expose the bounded installed fleet and current work backlog, rather than a single worker&#39;s perspective. - Current-control decision scope: Conductor can choose and revise work-to-child allocation, active agent count/model/folder, follow-up task instructions and interruption in response to observed fleet state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent user requests across projects, idle/busy/blocked children, failed tasks, approval pauses, delayed results and current model/account capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Conductor chooses whether to create or reuse an agent, what folder/model/account to assign, when to message or interrupt it and how to react to its returned result/status. - Decision owner: Conductor model running with the bundled role prompt and privileged first-party swarm MCP rights; runtime enforces access scopes and completion notifications. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listagents, startagent, sendmessage, readagent, task assignment, stop/interrupt, child-finish notifications, persistent fleet state and task dependency gates. - Closure path: User&#39;s multi-project goal→conductor lists fleet/task state→selects agent and assignment→agent acts→halay notifies its completion/error/block→conductor revises instructions, assignments or informs user. - Boundary reachability: A shipped Swarm chat has its own conductor role and tool permissions; unlike a passive dashboard it can model-select concrete task allocation from returned runtime state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party conductor prompt gives the model an operational portfolio mandate and action rights; subprocess state and database enforcement support rather than replace its choices. - Evidence: conductor mandate, scoped MCP actions, fleet/task state and wakeup, notification format. - Basis: Reachable first-party conductor perception→discretionary allocation/revision→child-action→status feedback over a bounded fleet. - Confidence: High for installed mandate and capability, not measured real fleet performance. - Caveats: The conductor&#39;s decision model runs via a required external CLI and may fail to act on feedback; human controls, context budgets and per-child permissions constrain it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent alternate evidence path produces an audit verdict returned to conductor beyond ordinary agent and tool reports. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False child completion claims and unsafe changes could be reviewed by humans; no separate installed auditing reviewer independently samples operational reality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Conductor can read child transcript, summaries and git status, but this is ordinary supervision; approvals are chosen by the human. - Decision owner: Conductor for monitoring and operator for approvals; no complementary auditor with independent judgment/return authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent transcript, worktree state, browser UI, status notifications, reviewable git changes and authentication. - Closure path: Child events→conductor/UI; no independent artifact sampling/replay→adversarial audit finding→corrective S3 loop in standard distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A separate transcript reader does not gain a distinct independent observational channel, and the human may optionally review but is outside first-party installed audit. - Evidence: swarm read/status, event handling. - Basis: Bounded absence review of strongest transcript/worktree evidence paths. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: External CLI vendors or human code review may supply external auditors, not credited to halay by default.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No continuous prospective environment analysis selects future capability/organizational changes for this fleet. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New CLI backends, future user work, schedule timing, model prices and capacity changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator configures accounts/backends and schedules; conductor handles current task choices and user-described future jobs. - Decision owner: Human operator for product/organizational evolution, conductor model for present tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Backend discovery, scheduled/recurring tasks, durable bots, peer configuration and model selection. - Closure path: User configuration/schedule→current or timed execution; no outside environmental forecast→strategic option selection→negotiation with current S3 and adopted future operating rule. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Scheduled execution and selectable backends are operational capacity, not autonomous anticipation of changing external circumstances. - Evidence: server and task schedule, conductor instructions, bots. - Basis: Bounded absence review including strongest persistent/scheduled mode. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Conductor can be asked to research future changes as a one-off S1 work item; no permanent S4 function follows.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No distinct legitimate ultimate identity-policy role owns and closes an organizational charter/mandate. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Privileged agent management, resource/cost limits, malicious agents, user identity/authentication and permission escalation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Authenticated human owner sets controls; static role access enforces conductor versus child scope, and the user decides tool approvals. - Decision owner: Operator for legitimate ultimate constraints, deterministic auth/MCP guard for enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Passkeys, per-agent bearer tokens, parent ownership checks, approval mode, model/account boundaries and persistent configuration. - Closure path: Operator setting or approval→guarded API behavior; no autonomous or parent-assisted identity-policy deliberation and returned authoritative mandate to S3/S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Strong security and an exclusive conductor role are not sufficient for an independent governance legitimacy cycle. - Evidence: auth/access, MCP scoping, server. - Basis: Bounded absence after screening legitimate operator policy and role privilege paths. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: A human-led institution could govern an installed halay fleet; it is not a supplied self-governing S5 actor.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0d074c28204362cf5ef61e4df5c16812954ee014/assessments/ha/halay.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0d074c28204362cf5ef61e4df5c16812954ee014\">0d074c2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T17:08:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lose4578/Auriga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Auriga</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lose4578/Auriga/tree/3e1be75e186e49f8c268fd5fc6c08427f99254ce\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3e1be75e186e49f8c268fd5fc6c08427f99254ce\">3e1be75 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: pursue robot-manipulation goals through visual observation, task planning, multi-stage preview/execution, measured action feedback and recovery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing object/robot geometry, failed or partial grasps, occlusion, movement slips, constrained arm trajectories and uncertain action completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next physical task action, observation, program, trajectory revision or recovery in light of returned evidence. - Decision owner: primary model-driven Codex actor (named Astra in optional verification documentation), with action authority mediated by the Harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: action identity and current-observation checks, native motion planner, gripper guards, single action writer, program review broker, action ledger, wall/budget supervision and the physical simulator. - Closure path: Codex task/plan → robotobserve and robotaction preview → model selects execute → Harness.execute native worker → measured receipt/images/stop reason → model adjusts or terminates episode. - Boundary reachability: normal gateway creates the Codex session and Harness, registers robotaction and connects execution to native RoboDojo worker. The model/tool loop is shipped with documented flags, not merely a benchmark test. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static robot safety gates do not select a new manipulation strategy; the actor model makes the task-level decisions even when a hard guard prevents unsafe execution. - Evidence: README execution loop, Harness, model-led operation. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct conflict-specific inter-S1 coordination and return is established in the supported robot actor organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dual-arm trajectory interference and concurrent program/native action requests are real actuator/resource hazards but not conflicts among distinct autonomous S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: arbitrate independent agent commitments and return a coordination resolution; not evidenced. - Decision owner: one actor plans both arms; deterministic Harness serializes physical actions and rejects stale plans, rather than an S2 coordination owner regulating competing agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stage-order dependencies, dual-arm path preview, active-writer exclusivity, plan revision, program broker approval and stop gates. - Closure path: native constraints and the primary actor can alter a manipulation task, but no separate inter-S1 interference-resolution result is fed into distinct operational agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: two robot arms are not independent goal-directed S1 cells; an optional evidence agent audits a claim but does not compete for physical task execution. - Evidence: unified actions, Harness action writer, program review. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no independent whole-organization current-control authority over a population of operational S1 commitments is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: native action budget, simulator step accounting, active action conflicts, unknown stop confirmation, model transport failures and episode wall limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select systemic allocation/priority/commitment intervention across distinct operations; absent beyond the single robot episode&#39;s work plan. - Decision owner: primary actor owns task strategy; Harness enforces state/timeout/cost constraints; operator defines task/benchmark configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: supervision.waitturn, action gate, per-action budget, program source grants, GPU resource checks and status/feedback records. - Closure path: failures can stop one episode or prompt its actor to try another action, not a separate whole-current S3 decision that regulates multiple autonomous units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: counters, stop gates and request retry policy are enforcement; removing a hypothetical S3 model leaves them intact. - Evidence: runtime supervision, Harness, model-led operation. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: the primary robot actor&#39;s local claim that a selected grasp/lift/transfer/place/release or retreat phase achieved its expected visible result, as distinct from a native worker&#39;s narrower completed execution receipt. - Ordinary reporting path: Harness.execute returns a native action receipt and current measured observation to the primary actor, which can then report success on the strength of its own readings; a tool response alone does not prove object retention or task completion. - Complementary access path: the separately launched Sol reviewer is given the raw linked BEFORE/AFTER permitted RGB images, measured robot state, immutable action IDs/receipts and the actor&#39;s intended outcome as a hypothesis; it checks visual identity/relation independently and returns a structured met/notmet/uncertain verdict with gaps. - Independence boundary: CodexEvidenceAgent creates a fresh ephemeral thread, empty workspace and isolated home, disables memories/tools/hooks, and rejects observed helper tool activity; it is not forked from the acting Codex history and cannot issue motion commands. The actor chooses checkpoint and evidence, limiting scope but not transferring visual judgment to the actor. - Who acts on findings: the primary Astra actor receives visionreview results in its ordinary tool stream and is instructed to resolve unmet/uncertain evidence with fresh observations or changed recovery; the reviewer itself cannot execute or compel a trajectory and the operating loop retains physical action ownership. - Function: provide an independent, evidence-limited second opinion on a manipulation operation&#39;s claimed visible outcome and send discrepancies or uncertainty back to the acting agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: optimistic success interpretations of completed motion receipts, wrong target identity, occlusion, unsupported grasp/placement/release claims and partial execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge met, notmet or uncertain for a claimed phase by comparing independent before/after RGB, execution receipts and measured robot state, and specify evidence gaps/recovery feedback. - Decision owner: separate fresh gpt-6.1-sol evidence agent; primary Astra remains owner of the subsequent physical action and the operator decides whether to enable this configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic provenance and schema checks, isolated fresh home/thread, suppressed tools/memory, payload source hashes, shared model budget, exact model assertion and unsupported-positive veto in constrainanswer. - Closure path: actor selects meaningful checkpoint and invokes visionreview(evidencemode=&#39;operation&#39;) → fresh Sol independently examines linked images/receipts and issues bounded verdict and follow-up → tool response returns to the primary actor&#39;s Codex turn → actor instructed to resolve uncertainty or choose a changed recovery before later motions. The auditor never executes robot actions. - Boundary reachability: features.visualreviewmodel=&#39;gpt-6.1-sol&#39; is a documented supported config; the actual Workbench instantiates VisualReviewer(CodexEvidenceAgent(session)), the runtime tool handler dispatches it and returns the structured assessment to the ordinary actor context. Default null mode has no S3 helper; positive vector reflects the first-party enabled operating mode, not default execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the fresh model provides the discretionary visual evidence judgment; deterministic validation limits unsupported assertions but cannot itself interpret ambiguous target retention/phase outcome. Actor authority to choose recovery is distinct from auditor judgment ownership. - Evidence: mode and return contract, Workbench construction, fresh agent isolation, review judgment, actor recovery instructions, tool handler. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: actor chooses evidence/request and need not obey a fixed compulsory verification phase sequence; this is a first-party supported optional independent second-opinion loop, not privileged simulator-ground-truth audit or guaranteed physical correction. No real provider/robot verification was executed here.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate externally prospective adaptation process with authorized whole-runtime capability-choice and return is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: novel object geometries, repeated failed grasps, changing camera viewpoints, different robot scenes and future task transfer. - Decisive decision or feedback right: change organizational operating capability based on prospective environmental alternatives; not evidenced at the assessed one-episode recursion. - Decision owner: primary model adapts task actions in the current scene; developers/operators configure perception services, model and robot plugins. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: adaptiveactioncheckpoint failure reminders, observation memory, trajectory cost previews, program synthesis and optional external perception. - Closure path: measured failures can prompt an immediate alternate grasp, viewpoint or trajectory within one task; no distinct environment/future analysis → approved organization-wide capability adaptation → feedback loop is implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: adaptive recovery within one operating episode is S1 learning/action selection, not automatically S4 strategic adaptation. - Evidence: checkpoint helper, model-led recovery, unified actions/memory. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no ultimate organizational policy/identity arbitration with legitimate authoritative return is implemented inside the robot episode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: robot safety boundaries, model permissions, execution modes, privileged task truth, tool grants and physical stop decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: adjudicate identity-level commitments and revise binding policy for the whole; not shown beyond fixed configuration and task controls. - Decision owner: developer/operator specifies guardrails and modes; deterministic Harness enforces validation; primary actor decides ordinary manipulation steps within those bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: actor observation redaction, action schema and state locks, program grants, model call budget and sandbox controls. - Closure path: individual actions are admitted/rejected against predefined rules, but no policy-level dispute → qualified authority → ultimate decision → new identity-governing operating rule is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model may choose a risky trajectory and receive advisory data, but it does not own the system&#39;s ultimate identity or change invariant control policy. - Evidence: action gate, actor information boundary, model-led operation. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ec6167d00afe015e5c030fd19139e421de731e13/assessments/au/auriga.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ec6167d00afe015e5c030fd19139e421de731e13\">ec6167d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-13T22:11:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/deepseek-ai/dsh-superagent-prototype\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">dsh-superagent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-13\">2026-11-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/deepseek-ai/dsh-superagent-prototype/tree/8c32a9dc5d72c033816c6a8110b37f40cf94fba4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8c32a9dc5d72c033816c6a8110b37f40cf94fba4\">8c32a9d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Complete a bounded repository work item through the worker&#39;s investigation, implementation and disposition. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Heterogeneous change requests, repository conditions, incoming feedback and task blockers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose how to investigate and implement the accepted change, whether new inputs fit the mandate, and what outcome the ticket can report. - Decision owner: The bundle-wired ticket worker session. The main session controls acceptance and routing, and the operator or repository maintainer retains external authorization and review rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-ticket workspaces and persistent DSH sessions, ticket mandates, tool bindings and structured report schemas. - Closure path: Acceptance creates the ticket and mandate, the executor launches the worker session, and the worker&#39;s report updates the assignment and determines whether work continues, awaits input or ends. A bound PR can keep the ticket open through review and closure. - Boundary reachability: The standard runtime wires worker tools and prompts into the session that the ticket executor launches. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the worker&#39;s judgment leaves launch and state handling but removes implementation choices and the resulting work. - Evidence: src/core/evals.ts, src/app/runtime.ts, src/app/tools/executor.ts, src/core/assignments.ts, prompts/worker.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: The result is scoped to authorized work. Inferred candidates only dispatch automatically in auto mode. Hosted DSH model behavior and maintainer acceptance are external.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Resolve ambiguous or overlapping ownership of follow-up work among ticket workers sharing a source thread. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A follow-up can reach several tickets with different mandates. A misplaced or duplicate assignment can make multiple workers act on the same request or leave it with the wrong worker. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Each affected worker judges whether to retain or hand back the follow-up. When ownership is released, the main session judges whether to attach it elsewhere, create distinct work or ignore it. - Decision owner: The shipped ticket worker roles and main orchestrator role, at their respective points in the hand-back loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Recorded trigger disposition and subscriptions, refusal of premature rerouting, requeue only after other ticket owners release the message, and an idempotent accept by trigger. - Closure path: A worker returns out-of-scope work with a reason. When no ticket still owns it, the next candidate carries the reasons and the main session can reroute. Attach supplies input to the selected ticket; accept creates a separate ticket. - Boundary reachability: Worker hand-back and main routing tools are registered by the runtime for active ticket and main sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runtime enforces the hand-back conditions. It does not choose whether a concrete follow-up fits a ticket&#39;s mandate or which ticket should receive released work. - Distinct S1 units: Concurrent ticket workers with independent mandates and persistent sessions in the same deployment. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Shared source threads can carry separate pieces of work and reach several ticket workers, with scope overlap or mistaken assignment of a new follow-up. - Attenuating coordination relation: Workers judge ownership and release misrouted input, and the main role can attach the released input to a better-fitting ticket using current ticket summaries. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The return reason becomes candidate context. The subsequent routing result changes which worker receives an actionable input and under which mandate it acts. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The return is tied to an identified ownership conflict across live units. The runtime blocks a second assignment while a ticket still owns the follow-up. - Evidence: docs/001-domain.md, docs/002-authorization.md, src/core/evals.ts, src/core/engine.ts, prompts/dispatch.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: This witness concerns scoped input ownership. It does not establish general file-conflict detection or contention-aware scheduling across arbitrary worktrees.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Regulate current deployment load and active commitments through operator decisions about concurrency, pause/resume and intervention on open tickets. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Queue pressure, stalled tickets, execution capacity and delivery problems across the deployment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Change the operating capacity or suspend/resume the deployment and intervene in live commitments from its current view. - Decision owner: Deployment operator. The main session can request a missing ticket report and routes individual work, while the executor enforces configured capacity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Overview health and ticket projections, revisioned settings, worker-slot accounting, ticket stop/retry commands and the processing pass. - Closure path: The operator reads active load and commitments, saves an executor setting or pauses/resumes processing, or stops/retries a ticket. The runtime uses the changed setting for subsequent work, or terminates/replaces the assignment. - Boundary reachability: The shipped panel reads overview and calls management routes on the same runtime and store that govern dispatch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The main role&#39;s local accountability action asks an idle worker for a report. Retry and control settings require explicit human direction. The supported autonomous role has no evidenced independent deployment-wide resource or intervention decision loop. - Whole-system current view: The deployment overview combines open tickets, work awaiting a person, executor load, configured capacity and active operational problems. - Current-control decision scope: Global dispatch capacity and processing state, with operator intervention in individual commitments from that whole view. - Evidence: src/app/views/overview.ts, src/app/http.ts, src/app/settings.ts, src/app/tools/main.ts, src/app/tools/main.ts, docs/003-execution.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Pause governs new evaluation, dispatch and delivery. It is distinct from stopping already-running work.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Complementary examination of operational claims through a separately scoped evidence and judgment path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A worker&#39;s ordinary report may omit or mischaracterize the implementation result. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No bundled independent actor was found that examines the actual work and returns an audit judgment to deployment control. - Decision owner: No first-party audit owner established in this deployment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Ticket and decision histories, model admission records, external PR review and check ingestion, and ordinary worker reports. - Closure path: External review/check feedback returns to the ticket worker. The main role consumes summaries and can request an ordinary missing report. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The admission judgment concerns prospective requests. PR reviewers are external actors. The worker&#39;s own report and its assessment of review feedback are operational reporting. - Evidence: prompts/dispatch.md, src/app/tools/main.ts, src/sources/github-trigger.ts, src/app/tools/executor.ts, docs/003-execution.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: External CI or reviewer evidence can affect implementation. The deployment supplies no independent audit owner or a separately configured first-party audit role.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Develop and return an adaptation option for the future capability of the deployment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changes in the deployment&#39;s environment that may call for a new capability or operating arrangement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No bundled loop was found that develops capability adaptation options from an external/prospective distinction and returns a decision to deployment control. - Decision owner: No first-party adaptation owner established at this recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Source observation and replay, observe mode, model and source settings, and versioned operator prompt configuration. - Closure path: Observations become ticket decisions, including prospective accepts in observe mode. An operator can enable auto or edit settings and prompts using the management surface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The main role assesses whether a concrete request fits current rules. The observe-to-auto recommendation is an operator deployment calibration step, with prospective ticket records rather than generated alternatives for changing deployment capability. - Evidence: docs/002-authorization.md, src/policy.ts, src/app/tools/main.ts, src/app/prompts.ts, SETUP.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: The operator can use external analysis to change configuration. The boundary is the deployment&#39;s shipped operational loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Establish and revise the standing mandate for which repository demand the deployment may take up. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting or changing expectations about who may authorize work, which classes of changes are in scope, and whether inferred work may be undertaken. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the deployment-wide admission policy, allowed authorities, rule scope and mode. - Decision owner: The deployment operator, acting directly through the panel or by explicit configuration instruction. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Policy and mode schema, settings revision, computed policy version, admission checks, affected-ticket notifications and later mandate revalidation. - Closure path: The operator changes the standing mandate after examining decision records or deployment needs. The saved version changes subsequent evaluation, queues stale evaluations anew, and sends relevant changes to affected active tickets. The executor revalidates ended work before resuming under its mandate. - Boundary reachability: The standard deployment exposes policy settings and decision records. Both the panel and explicit-request tool save through the runtime settings service read by evaluation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The main role applies the standing policy. Its settings tool is scoped to explicit human configuration requests, and assessed source content does not authorize a change in policy. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: The deployment&#39;s enduring authorization envelope for proactive repository work, beyond any individual ticket approval. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: The deployment operator sets the policy, including in auto mode. - Return-to-operation path: Versioned settings feed the next admission decisions and revalidation; relevant changes are delivered to affected live workers. - Evidence: docs/002-authorization.md, src/app/tools/main.ts, src/app/settings.ts, src/core/engine.ts. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: A policy change does not immediately revoke all active mandates. Its effects depend on scope, notifications and the next decision or revalidation path.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/07848cd39bc6ed0eb563c11c2910fd627ec27ca6/assessments/ds/dsh-superagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 07848cd39bc6ed0eb563c11c2910fd627ec27ca6\">07848cd &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T17:57:28+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mihazs/agent-harness-lab\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent Harness Lab</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mihazs/agent-harness-lab/tree/1b165594818bab9f92605fb4bd5ec0c6f943c2fc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1b165594818bab9f92605fb4bd5ec0c6f943c2fc\">1b16559 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: investigate bounded harness defects and generate an executable candidate instruction/skill change against project evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown agent failure mechanisms, contradictory observations, ineffective instructions, regressions and source-context gaps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the proposer model chooses which supported mechanism to address and what candidate file edits to stage; baseline/candidate model task executors choose actions under identical frozen tasks and runtime settings. - Decision owner: selected Codex/Claude/Responses model-backed proposer and task roles in explicitly invoked improve mode; first-party controller scopes and executes them. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project-local snapshots and path allowlists, model role prompts, source/analysis receipts, artifact hashes, shared trial/time ceiling, frozen candidate, paired executors and evaluation feedback. - Closure path: collect bounded evidence → proposer model selects candidate → change is frozen → baseline and candidate agent trials execute → observed failures/regressions feed the bounded refinement or final no-change/recommendation. - Boundary reachability: workflow.py invokes native/Responses role runner in preparation and task phases; ahl improve is the documented CLI entry, provided runtime availability and permissions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: proposer decides semantic repair hypotheses and actual candidate contents, not just a deterministic suggested text; analyze, static classifiers and test bookkeeping alone would be constructors, not S1 A. - Evidence: proposer dispatch and trial flow, controlled role preparation, documented modes. - Basis: pinned first-party execution code and runtime contract; not a live paid Codex/Claude experiment here. - Confidence: high that model-backed roles are shipped, medium for real-world repair quality. - Caveats: no model calls in analyze or offline diagnostics; no general automatic promotion implied by successful paired tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no separately established inter-S1 reciprocal conflict-attenuation loop beyond task role isolation and experiment scheduling at the defined single-run boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a proposer could contaminate evaluator inputs or a concurrent improvement run could alter the same project, but AHL prevents those situations by frozen views/locks rather than demonstrated feedback among independently operating S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: controller grants separate role paths, budgets, frozen snapshots and project locks; a stale or leaked input blocks the whole evaluation. - Decision owner: deterministic custody/permission controller, without cross-worker conflict-specific negotiation or corrective return to both S1 cells. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role-scoped views, frozen task banks and hashes, project lock, candidate/baseline isolation, source-drift checks, sequential trial orchestration. - Closure path: potential cross-role contamination is prevented or detected; invalid runs stop or require intervention. This is evaluation integrity, not evidenced coordination of independent operational commitments. - Boundary reachability: role views, frozen candidate and validation are shipped; a separate correction negotiation between proposer and evaluator is not. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct fresh roles, sequential stages and file isolation are important safeguards, but not enough to infer VSM S2 without a concrete disturbance-feedback relation returning to affected S1 units. - Evidence: role construction and separation, controlled execution boundaries, preflight/custody policy. - Basis: bounded negative review of multi-role experiment/custody paths. - Confidence: medium-high for this recursion. - Caveats: a future multi-project adaptation fleet could implement true S2; that is outside this single-project run.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: regulate the active run&#39;s preparation/evaluation roles, resource budget, candidate eligibility, current evidence and terminal disposition, with user-owned runtime/scope initiation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: budget exhaustion, changed source files, invalid evaluator controls, failing task bank, unavailable authentication, partial runs and repeated monitoring without new evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: controller freezes/validates candidate and grader inputs, schedules tests/refinement, rejects a candidate or emits recommendations; human owner authorizes project, chosen runtime, scope and any later behavioral adoption. - Decision owner: deterministic workflow.py and promotion prerequisites (C), with human operator (P) initiating and deciding outside-CLI adoption. Controller does not quietly grant itself publication rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project locks, shared 45-minute/24-role defaults, checksums, state receipts, role budgets, graded trial coverage, early fail-closed exits, optional monitor checkpoints. - Closure path: current evidence and role result → controller authorizes next preparation/test/stop or reports unsupported → stored checkpoint/receipt constrains the next agent dispatch. - Boundary reachability: ahl improve and ahl watch use these live controller functions; watch is foreground and must be explicitly started. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-run scheduling/disposition is rule-based despite model roles doing task work; real human controls authenticated invocation and reviewed candidate adoption. - Whole-system current view: baseline/candidate hashes, source identity, proposer/evaluator states, task bank coverage, model calls, time/trial remaining, results and previous monitor checkpoints. - Current-control decision scope: allow/deny a role run, protect frozen inputs, retry once, stop on invalid evidence, write final review/recommendation and watch restart suppression. - Evidence: controller loop, invocation preflight, monitoring mode. - Basis: first-party deterministic role/state controller. - Confidence: high in structural control rights; actual credentialed runs not repeated here. - Caveats: human adoption beyond CLI and real trusted production integration are not claimed as observed automatic completion.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: a separately prompted evaluator-author agent constructs tests/graders from baseline/source evidence while deliberately blind to the candidate, and the controller executes those frozen evaluators to challenge the proposer&#39;s claimed improvement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: proposer self-praise, overfit instruction rewrites, candidate-specific tests, evaluator leakage and differences that disappear on held-out/regression cases. - Decisive decision or feedback right: evaluator-author model independently chooses test cases and grader construction within the baseline evidence and broken/partial/complete control contract; frozen graders then measure actual paired task outputs and can cause rejection or bounded refinement. - Decision owner: separate fresh model-backed evaluator-author role owns substantive test design; deterministic comparison controller enforces controls and verdict/disposition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate role workspace/views, baseline-only source context, withheld candidate and held-out inputs, grader self-tests, manifest freezing, task result artifacts, same-runtime paired trials and custody checks. - Closure path: proposer freezes candidate/claim → independently scoped author designs tests without seeing patch → controls/graders are frozen → baseline/candidate execute → actual result comparison rejects/permits refinement or emits review recommendation. - Boundary reachability: workflow.py constructs a separate evaluator session in native mode or construction.author/controlled role access, and validates a manifest before paired trials. No optional uninstalled reviewer service is required beyond the chosen agent runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deciding what independent probes should measure involves a different model context, not simply running a copy of the proposer&#39;s test; the final grader/veto itself is deterministic, so this is not automatically a certified independent human scientific audit. - Claim being audited: a proposed harness instruction/skill edit improves required task outcomes without regression under comparable inputs. - Ordinary reporting path: proposer mechanisms, candidate diff, self-declared proposal.json and any development trial feedback. - Complementary access path: evaluator author receives baseline/source/analysis without candidate patch, builds unrelated executable tasks and graders, and the controller examines fresh baseline/candidate task artifacts. - Independence boundary: roles are separately scoped and patch-blind by construction, but cooperative Codex/Claude runtime/hook behavior and model provider isolation are not proven adversarial security; some source metadata is shared. - Who acts on findings: controller rejects/refines/recommends; human decides any behavioral adoption. No claim of certified unconditional promotion. - Evidence: independent evaluation-construction prompt and calls, role-specific controlled author, frozen task method. - Basis: explicit shipped two-role model construction plus executable artifact feedback, not observed scientific effect size. - Confidence: medium-high for independent input view and actual verdict path, medium for model quality and native role isolation. - Caveats: “fresh session” alone is not a proof of full root independence or test correctness; graders need explicit self-tests and absence of leakage.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no completed behavioral adoption return turning evaluated future-harness candidates into active project operating capability under the shipped CLI&#39;s general workflow. - Disturbance / variety regulated: evolving task types and systematic future agent behavior might warrant new harness instructions and skills. - Decisive decision or feedback right: proposer can forecast candidate effects and evaluation compares development/regression/held-out tasks, but improve deliberately returns a review recommendation instead of applying behavioral changes; the human may later edit active files separately. - Decision owner: user/project outside the CLI owns actual behavioral promotion; no first-party general S4 decision-to-adoption loop has authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: retrospective analysis, selected candidate proposals, prospective task banks, held-out controls, promotion prerequisites and fail-closed recommendation status. - Closure path: candidate → prospective paired task evidence → review recommendation; missing final behavioral activation in the same supported controller means the full outside-and-then capability return is unclosed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: creating and evaluating future options is substantial S4-adjacent intelligence but cannot be scored as completed S4 when the proposed capability is explicitly not adopted. Narrow structural BOM repair is not general behavioral S4. - Evidence: README no auto-adoption, promotion policy recommendation-only, one-call narrow repair. - Basis: explicit runtime/README negative authority and supported-mode examination. - Confidence: high for CLI&#39;s deliberate blocked behavioral return; a manual fallback may differ under explicit user authority. - Caveats: installed harness-self-improvement skill describes assisted evaluation/editing; without a validated matching runtime/receipt this is not imported as general autonomous promotion into the guarded ahl improve path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate whole-project identity/ultimate-purpose adjudication loop evidenced in one harness optimization run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: privacy of traces, scope of external models, credentialed cost, whether an evaluator can be trusted and changes to global instructions are real governance concerns. - Decisive decision or feedback right: invocation permissions and configured scope restrict project files and runtime capabilities, while the user decides model/provider, accepts adoption and can stop the command; none is charter-level identity resolution returned as binding system purpose. - Decision owner: outside user/project authority; controller enforces safety/eligibility preconditions rather than deliberating ultimate purpose. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project path allowlists, no global skills by default, no publication rights, credential-bound external calls, privacy-safe artifacts and explicit fail-closed prerequisites. - Closure path: invalid permission/role scope blocks the run or preserves unmodified files; no first-party identity dispute reaches a higher legitimate policy owner and returns as an ultimate revised charter. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: safety and provenance constraints limit experiments but are not themselves S5 governance. - Evidence: scope and limits, preflight boundaries, promotion authority. - Basis: bounded negative review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the human project owner has external policy rights; those are not automatically first-party runtime S5(P).\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/19d30e2238e1d50e87e240bcf67ab7b02ca7f8f6/assessments/ag/agent-harness-lab.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 19d30e2238e1d50e87e240bcf67ab7b02ca7f8f6\">19d30e2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:08:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJUZhiyuCai/bread-companion\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bread Companion</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJUZhiyuCai/bread-companion/tree/058ad153e27bd6470a7e5ef4cab778a93d659a6d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 058ad153e27bd6470a7e5ef4cab778a93d659a6d\">058ad15 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomous bounded conversation and evidence-task transformation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: different user material, missing/conflicting evidence, rejected interpretations and malformed or inappropriate generated candidates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose supported findings, wording, draft structure or a practice question; revise after concrete rejection/validation feedback. - Decision owner: the configured real-agent model within the first-party generation/repair loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mode prompts, selected source segments, schema/evidence/style validators, two-attempt repair, run IDs and client lifecycle checks. - Closure path: validation errors re-enter a new generator attempt; accepted content becomes current findings/card/practice state, influencing later turns. - Boundary reachability: real-agent.generate implements the provider interface used by the deployed server; BreadController submits a current turn and reducer applies only matching epoch/context results. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model determines substantive task content and correction, while the controller enforces validity and human save authority. Fixed UI transitions alone could not produce the same discretionary outcomes. - Evidence: server/providers/real-agent.ts; prompts/agent.ts; domain/evidence.ts; services/bread-controller.ts; domain/reducer.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: no general tool-executing autonomy; drafts require user confirmation for persistence. This is not a therapy efficacy or clinical safety claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct operational-unit coordination path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These are one conversational work cell&#39;s pipeline/transport stages and revision-consistency controls. No distinct purposeful operational S1 cells with an inter-S1 disturbance/attenuation relation were found. - Evidence: server/providers/real-agent.ts; services/run-controller.ts; services/call-controller.ts; domain/reducer.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-current organizational control function established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These regulate individual requests, versions, service capacity and task consent. A session view or stop button is not by itself whole-system resource/commitment regulation with a function-specific parent decision loop. - Evidence: services/bread-controller.ts; services/run-controller.ts; domain/reducer.ts; server/limits.ts; server/admission.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: narrow complementary semantic audit of whether contested analysis reintroduces the user&#39;s denied interpretation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a candidate can pass schema/verbatim/lexical checks yet paraphrase or recommend the rejected interpretation again. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently compare actual candidate with user rejection record and judge semantic consistency. - Decision owner: the model in the fresh rejection-review invocation; runtime parses/enforces its verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate review prompt/request, only candidate/rejection/current user data, bounded audit input, conservative verdict parsing and forced retry/fallback. - Closure path: inconsistent/invalid audit adds REJECTEDINTERPRETATIONREPEATED; generator receives explicit corrective feedback; after a second repeated rejection the product returns quote/unknown fallback rather than display the contested interpretation. - Boundary reachability: RealAgentProvider invokes the separate reviewer only for non-listen analysis with rejectedFindings after normal validation; reducer REJECT and context-builder persist/send that rejection record. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the independent review call supplies semantic judgment beyond the generator&#39;s candidate and deterministic lexical check; parsing is enforcement, not the owner. No independent provider identity is claimed. - Evidence: server/providers/real-agent.ts; server/providers/rejection-review.ts; domain/reducer.ts; domain/context-builder.ts; domain/evidence.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: same configured model/provider may serve both roles; independence is fresh context and task separation for this narrow consistency claim, not empirical independence from shared model biases. Ordinary chat is unaudited. - Claim being audited: the candidate respects the user&#39;s previously rejected interpretation and its paraphrases. - Ordinary reporting path: generator candidate passes schema/evidence/style and exact-normalized rejection checks. - Complementary access path: fresh semantic comparison of the actual full candidate against persisted user rejection records. - Independence boundary: separate stateless request with dedicated review instructions and no generator hidden reasoning/history; source of correction is user record, not a generator self-report. - Who acts on findings: the provider&#39;s repair loop withholds the candidate, requests revision and eventually substitutes a traceable non-interpretive fallback.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no prospective capability-adaptation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These adjust current task content, context and service availability. They do not develop future environment/capability options and return an adaptation decision that changes the harness&#39;s present repertoire. Research/benchmark plans are adjacent. - Evidence: domain/context-builder.ts; domain/preferences.ts; server/providers/real-agent.ts; services/bread-controller.ts; docs/product-harness.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity or ultimate-policy decision/return path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static identity/permissions and user authority over task/content/save do not supply ultimate-policy arbitration. No identity-level issue is routed to a legitimate ultimate decision owner and returned as a new governing policy. - Evidence: prompts/agent.ts; domain/policy.ts; domain/reducer.ts; services/bread-controller.ts; docs/product-harness.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8c0178fd561849a1d61ba41f63780227cb755b15/assessments/br/bread-companion.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8c0178fd561849a1d61ba41f63780227cb755b15\">8c0178f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T22:54:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jirathip-dev/herdsman\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">herdsman</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jirathip-dev/herdsman/tree/efd970e817c2021439c5415739e695f4af119b1d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision efd970e817c2021439c5415739e695f4af119b1d\">efd970e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: configured coding agent lanes implement and repair requested changes; separately tasked review agents exercise real task-specific judgment on code/tests. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new issue requirements, unknown codebase state, implementation errors, test failures and review findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which code and tests to change, what fixes to make and how to respond with evidence to reviewer findings within the owner-authorized issue. - Decision owner: model-based implementer and, for their bounded verification output, reviewer roles launched under the fleet pipeline; the deterministic drive merely chooses when to launch the next stage. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: herdr worktree/pane spawn, issue brief, role prompts, profile hooks, PR cache, head-pinned verdicts and event log. - Closure path: issue queued → implementer writes/tests/pushes change → reviewer evaluates exact head → FAIL returns findings to fix lane or PASS and green CI lead through merge gate → code reaches integration branch. - Boundary reachability: drive and timers launch the roles by supported fleet settings, and the named default pipeline wires their outputs back to future steps; this is not a unit-test-only role. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: code edits, targeted tests and adversarial judgments require meaningful agent choices; herdsman&#39;s hard transition and merge checks are constructor enforcement, not additional autonomous S1 judgment. - Evidence: role instructions, spawn source, pipeline. - Basis: documented supported operation plus pinned role and spawn wiring. - Confidence: high for model role construction, bounded for outcomes without a provider-backed run in this review. - Caveats: no agent executes without an installed, authenticated external harness and herdr; no unattended intelligence is attributed to the Rust scheduler itself.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: first-party conflict preview, serial integration gate and isolated lane worktrees attenuate specific interference among concurrently coding issue units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two issue agents editing overlapping files or branches cause rejected merges, overwrite/ordering hazards and repeated updates against the integration branch. - Decisive decision or feedback right: detect file-level collisions with git merge-tree, show the specific conflicting paths, order ready PRs, enforce required serial groups and return the losing lane to update its branch before another reviewed merge. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic overlap, drive, gate and serial-group rules; no model has a standing right to settle portfolio-wide branch conflicts by changing these policies. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated worktrees, cached head-pair collision preview, HEADS-UP prompts, exact-head verdicts, serial gate and update rounds with bounded retry. - Closure path: conflicting concurrent heads → warning/serial ordering and first merge → implementer of later head receives update instruction and incorporates integration → fresh reviewer verdict and CI → next eligible merge. - Boundary reachability: the installed drive calls overlap preview before action ordering, and the supported conflict/behind path actively spawns an update role. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: concrete cross-issue contention is treated, but collision detection and merge precedence are coded invariants, not an autonomous S2 decision owner; this is C. - Distinct S1 units: independently running implementer lanes for different issue worktrees in the same configured fleet. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping file edits and stale branch ancestry can make otherwise valid S1 output conflict at integration. - Attenuating coordination relation: file-level preview and first-ready ordering, strict serial marking, integration update and mandatory fresh review. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: later implementer gets exact conflicting paths/updated integration instructions, edits its work and pushes a changed head for another review. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the relation recognizes actual write-set/merge interference between independent S1 agents and closes that conflict through a change in what the later agent does. - Evidence: overlap source, drive conflicts and ordering, lane conflict/update contract. - Basis: directly implemented collision-specific runtime path and prompt feedback. - Confidence: high for structural conflict attenuation; no claim of perfect prediction or autonomous negotiation. - Caveats: an early heads-up is advisory and does not lock all overlapping files; a first reviewed merge may still force an update round.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: the human fleet owner sees the live issue/lane/host portfolio and can pause or resume a fleet, intervene in blocked work and decide current resource and merge envelopes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: excessive concurrent lanes, host pressure, stalled fixes, owner questions and unwanted work on active issues. - Decisive decision or feedback right: use the phone board/CLI to pause or resume, move/park an issue, answer owner asks, extend review rounds or grant the narrow owner override without bypassing exact-head review and CI. - Decision owner: fleet owner/operator. drive computes the next mechanically valid transition, but does not set purpose/priorities as an AI metasystem. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live status, board and need-you cards, logged pause/decision events, queue moves, host admission and owner-only intervention controls. - Closure path: operator inspects current state → changes fleet pause/queue/decision or permitted round limit → next drive/kick reads event state and changes or withholds operational lane work. - Boundary reachability: pause, resume, ask, answer and board actions are shipped CLI/server paths and drive reads the same event log, not aspirational UI only. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the human selects priorities and discretionary intervention; programmed drive and resource thresholds do not upgrade to S3 A. - Whole-system current view: board shows queue, pipeline, lanes, host load, waiting and needs-you across a fleet. - Current-control decision scope: current issue order, which work waits or proceeds, review rounds, host/cost envelope and capacity within configured policy. - Evidence: owner board and drive behavior, live host control, pause/answer implementation. - Basis: explicit human command path and recorded return to scheduler. - Confidence: high for supported owner mode; model-directed supervisor not established. - Caveats: UI requires a running server/paired device; same control also exists through CLI.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independent, adversarial reviewer agents inspect implementation code and objective acceptance evidence outside the implementer&#39;s self-report. - Disturbance / variety regulated: plausible but incorrect implementation claims, tests proving only mocks, leaked secrets and unreviewed scope changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: read the actual diff and criteria, independently rerun tests and return a head-specific PASS/FAIL with blocking findings that the implementer must address. - Decision owner: a separately launched read-only reviewer model in a distinct lane and role/profile; verdict and CI checks merely enforce validity and preserve its findings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dedicated reviewer worktree, read-only pre-tool hook, verdict.md with reviewed 40-hex head and tests checked, exact-head gate and fresh check-round spawn. - Closure path: implementation PR head → reviewer inspects raw code/tests/criteria independently → head-pinned verdict and findings → implementer fix or approved integration → new head invalidates prior verdict and is independently reviewed again. - Boundary reachability: default and custom pipelines include independent check stages, distinct from build, with verified role constraints and drive spawns on pushed heads. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: adversarial technical judgment belongs to a distinct model role that can reject claims, not a boolean test wrapper or the implementer&#39;s own confirmation. - Claim being audited: implementation actually meets issue acceptance criteria and tests/PR description prove it. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer diff, test report and PR claim. - Complementary access path: independent checkout/diff, direct tests and head evidence against the original issue brief. - Independence boundary: reviewer role is prohibited from writing code, commits, pushes and PR comments, uses its own lane and verifies the actual current head; the same external harness vendor may still provide both models. - Who acts on findings: the subsequent implementer fix lane; herdsman&#39;s event/gate machine blocks merge until fresh PASS and green CI. - Evidence: reviewer role, review stage isolation, verdict source. - Basis: mandatory shipped review workflow and access separation. - Confidence: high for implemented audit path; reviewer accuracy not measured here. - Caveats: separate role/worktree provides process and tool isolation, not external institutional independence; no live paid provider review was performed in this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective outside-environment interpretation and return into enduring fleet capability changes was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new coding models, repository ecosystems, infrastructure economics and future workloads might eventually require adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no internal future-environment option selection and promotion right evidenced; host choices and code paths are bounded to present work/load. - Decision owner: not established for a separate S4 loop; fleet configuration and infrastructure upgrade choices remain with humans. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: host-history sampling, cost ceiling, doctor, backlog, context prompts and issue queue. - Closure path: observed present host pressure can deny a lane or start a configured cloud overflow host, but no projected external future becomes a selected enduring fleet strategy returned to implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: capacity observation is near-term S3/operational admission, and repository improvement by contributors is outside the installed fleet recursion. - Evidence: host placement and budget, architecture and scope. - Basis: bounded negative source review of shipped operating modes. - Confidence: medium-high for missing S4 closure in the reviewed modes. - Caveats: future model/host choice by the operator is possible but not credited as installed autonomous S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party loop was established that brings an identity- or ultimate-policy-level issue to legitimate authority and returns the binding result to govern the fleet as a coherent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing the fleet&#39;s ultimate mission, reconciling external future adaptation with current obligations, or revising the constitutional distribution of authority could require such a loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the owner may edit fleet.toml and decide scoped owner asks, but no shipped identity-level agenda or decision-return path was evidenced beyond those task and administration actions. - Decision owner: not established for S5 at the declared fleet recursion; ultimate responsibility remains with the human owner outside the demonstrated herdsman function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: owner/routine ask classifier, OWNERTOPIC refusal, no-default owner answer, protected branches, command hooks, limit settings and fleet configuration. - Closure path: owner decisions are returned to individual tasks and settings bind future runs, but there is no demonstrated loop from an organization-level identity dispute through deliberation to a revised ultimate charter applied by the fleet. - Boundary reachability: task-level owner asks and configuration commands are shipped; their existence is not by itself S5 closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: approval for secrets, cost or scope during a single implementation may be important safety governance without constituting identity/policy-level VSM S5. - Evidence: owner/routine decision contract, ask/answer code, fleet policy and gate. - Basis: bounded negative first-party review, distinguishing task authorization from ultimate-policy closure. - Confidence: medium-high for this scoped absence; an independently organized human governance layer is not ruled out. - Caveats: this does not deny that an operator can govern or change herdsman outside the built fleet workflow.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2500cd237846b78303b459e41ab53d20a64b0f13/assessments/he/herdsman.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2500cd237846b78303b459e41ab53d20a64b0f13\">2500cd2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T17:39:48+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Beyon-Digital/control-plane\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Beyon Control Plane</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Beyon-Digital/control-plane/tree/9dbe4012bdf8b2d2ee333402912b408aadd35e6d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9dbe4012bdf8b2d2ee333402912b408aadd35e6d\">9dbe401 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: provider coding agents work on repo tasks through native worker session and tool/result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code, compilation failures, tool restrictions, interrupted provider processes and changing workspace state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each configured provider agent chooses coding actions and follow-up responses in its provider session, limited by its own approval policy and worker exposure; the first-party worker accepts, persists and reports concrete execution events. - Decision owner: model-backed Codex/Claude/Kiro agent; external provider inference is a runtime dependency, while Beyon owns the adapter, execution and feedback boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider stdio/sidecar adapters, scoped session creation, event stream, workspace tools, idempotency receipts, device authorization and abort. - Closure path: prompt is accepted by worker → provider agent works with permitted tools → worker persists events and file effects → operator/orchestrator reads outcome and can issue follow-up or cancellation. - Boundary reachability: README documents installed providers and worker start; orchestrator&#39;s concrete createsession and promptsession routes call authenticated v1/sessions endpoints. The provider must separately be installed/authenticated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the provider agent selects how to do coding work rather than simply replaying deterministic scripts; host safety and networking alone are not the autonomous decision. - Evidence: run and provider boundary, provider adapters, orchestrator session calls. - Basis: source and supported deployment documentation; no live provider inference run. - Confidence: high for reachability; real account/provider integration is not independently proven. - Caveats: the external agents&#39; internal organizational functions are not copied into first-party Beyon ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no evidenced inter-S1 conflict-specific reciprocal attenuation loop between independently acting coding agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independent sessions could edit shared files or compete for worker resources, but the reviewed implementation does not show a first-party overlap analyzer and corrective return to the competing operational units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the orchestrator&#39;s tool calls are sequential and worker policies bound sessions/concurrency; neither explicitly identifies contested S1 obligations and reconciles them with collision feedback. - Decision owner: no distinct inter-S1 coordination owner established; session limits are deterministic worker enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sequential model calls, per-account isolation, role grants, session ownership, worker quotas, Git conditional saves and separate worktrees. - Closure path: requests are authenticated and serialized locally where required; a resource denial returns an error, not an evidenced negotiation/adjustment between multiple S1 agents. - Boundary reachability: concurrency and host/account controls are shipped but do not themselves prove inter-S1 regulatory coordination. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: having many sessions, a central orchestrator and an action cap is insufficient without identified actual collisions and a feedback relation specifically resolving those collisions. - Evidence: orchestrator tool loop, worker controls, workspace operations. - Basis: bounded first-party negative inspection. - Confidence: medium; a model might manually notice a conflict, but a reproducible supported collision workflow is not demonstrated. - Caveats: explicit separate worktrees reduce collision probability; they do not automatically constitute S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: a client-side main orchestrator can inspect the present portfolio of installed agents, active sessions, progress and host account capabilities, and direct current coding work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tasks accepted but not completed, unavailable providers, failed/uncertain sessions, unexpected file effects, new user priorities and permission changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: when management is enabled, the main model can select a provider, create sessions, prompt them and interrupt a live run after querying hostinfo, listsessions, sessionevents, workspacediff and other evidence. Human retains scope and approval rights. - Decision owner: separate model-backed first-party OrchestratorEngine for operational session-portfolio decisions; human owner supplies binding agent-management permission, host/account selection and provider/tool approvals. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scoped API client, model tool catalog/dispatcher allowlist, per-turn 10-round/40-call budget, user toggle, persistent worker session events and host access checks. - Closure path: current host/session/workspace evidence → orchestrator chooses create/prompt/interrupt or wait/inspect → worker accepts/rejects → event states return to subsequent model judgment and user UI. - Boundary reachability: OrchestratorEngine.run calls configured model inference and OrchestratorTools.execute implements actual endpoints, available from the native Orchestrator screen; read-only mode removes management calls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current portfolio management is an explicit model decision layer distinct from a worker&#39;s deterministic dispatch; notation includes (P) because the user holds the management enablement and unapproved tool authority. - Whole-system current view: worker host/provider discovery, per-session states/events, files and Git state across the connected account. - Current-control decision scope: creation, follow-up prompting, interruption and current inspection; not identity policy, user consent overrides or provider permission approval. - Evidence: executable tool catalog and dispatch, orchestrator limits and scope. - Basis: first-party model loop and scoped live API wiring; not a successful real model-provider performance claim. - Confidence: medium-high for implemented mode, with uncertainty about account entitlement and real deployment operation. - Caveats: without a connected worker or with management disabled, this S3 agent&#39;s action right is absent; these modes are not silently credited with full orchestration.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: provide evidence independent of coding-agent prose about actual session acceptance/completion, worker state and Git working-tree differences, enabling a corrective decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a coding agent can claim a task is done when a prompt was only accepted or its changes are missing; client state can be stale after disconnect/restart. - Decisive decision or feedback right: orchestrator/human can query durable worker sessionevents, listsessions and raw workspacediff, compare to the coding agent&#39;s story and then change follow-up or interrupt; the alternative evidence path itself is deterministic rather than a dedicated autonomous auditor. - Decision owner: independent first-party worker event/actual-file evidence constructor (C); model orchestrator or human interprets it, but the code does not guarantee a distinct adversarial auditing-model verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable SQLite events and receipts, restart uncertain rather than success/replay, actual workspace Git diff endpoint, clear prompt acceptance semantics. - Closure path: provider report/accepted prompt → fresh worker events and raw Git diff challenge the claimed state → main orchestrator/user can send a correction or halt a session → next provider action and events reflect the adjustment. - Boundary reachability: model tool catalog includes these live read-only tools, backed by worker API and stored events; the docs explicitly instruct inspection rather than treating acceptance as completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: these observations do not originate in the coding agent&#39;s natural-language assertion, yet no separate reviewer model automatically produces a verdict; hence a complementary constructor, not S3 A. - Claim being audited: the provider agent actually completed requested work and current workspace effects match its claimed progress. - Ordinary reporting path: provider messages, prompt-accepted notifications and local client conversation state. - Complementary access path: persisted host event cursor/status and independent Git filesystem diff read through the authenticated worker. - Independence boundary: coding agents can change workspace files and might manipulate their own outputs; the event store is separately maintained by the worker, but does not prove correctness, and actual Git diff alone cannot validate tests. - Who acts on findings: parent human or client-side orchestrator issues a new prompt, pauses, or explicitly declines completion claims. - Evidence: orchestrator independent read tools, durable events and restart semantics, worker store. - Basis: first-party separate observation and optional corrective return, not an observed independent semantic audit. - Confidence: medium: robust source separation, discretionary rather than mandatory corrective use. - Caveats: an orchestrator can choose not to inspect, and a diff can miss semantics; no guaranteed code-review safety threshold is inferred.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no packaged externally prospective scenario/option selection feeding durable changes to future operating capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future agent/provider availability, model eligibility, host topology and longer-term developer workflows may shift. - Decisive decision or feedback right: humans select current model profiles/endpoints, connect hosts and install workers; model discovery and account authorization are present-day capability choices, not a strategic outside-and-then return loop. - Decision owner: developer/operator configuration; no distinct first-party S4 intelligence decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model profiles and discovery, platform release/updater, host management, installation and verification guides. - Closure path: a user can switch a model or host and run the current orchestrator, but there is no shown future environment options assessment that binds a changed long-term operating capability through S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: discovering current models and installing signed updates are not VSM S4 merely because they concern technological change. - Evidence: model setup, architecture and update trust. - Basis: bounded negative review of shipped model/settings, current orchestrator and future release modes. - Confidence: medium-high at declared host/account recursion. - Caveats: developer roadmap and update mechanisms can evolve without proving a deployed prospective adaptation regulator.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no evidenced ultimate identity/purpose policy conflict adjudicated by a legitimate organizational authority and returned to runtime commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicting device trust, human permission and long-term assistant identity may require parent policy, but runtime currently applies already configured controls. - Decisive decision or feedback right: administrators enroll/revoke devices and grant roles; the user enables agent management, accepts provider permissions and can emergency-stop. These are scoped operational/security rights, not identity-policy adjudication. - Decision owner: outside human user/account administrator and project governance; no shipped distinct S5 decision loop with a charter-level return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TLS device identity, role/session ACL, certificate renewal, worker privilege boundaries, explicit trusted mode and emergency stop. - Closure path: permission changes apply to future API calls and revoke streams, without a witnessed foundational identity dispute being raised, decided and returned as ultimate policy across the organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: revocation and human override are genuine safeguards but not sufficient S5 closure; no autonomous S5 is inferred from a secure product name or operator veto. - Evidence: trust model and deployment limits, human agent-management bounds. - Basis: bounded negative inspection of actual governance/permissions rather than contributor organization. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: multi-user administrators can govern security policies, but that is not an evidenced question-to-legitimate-S5-to-binding-operational-return loop at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8ba36072b4c05023dce5352227d9a0bad1958d31/assessments/be/beyon-control-plane.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8ba36072b4c05023dce5352227d9a0bad1958d31\">8ba3607 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-13T21:22:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mdiri-ai/SoduWork\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SoduWork</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-11\">2026-11-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mdiri-ai/SoduWork/tree/97b9c2118a966869a862ef3aa3181ccb77c3dd3b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 97b9c2118a966869a862ef3aa3181ccb77c3dd3b\">97b9c21 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement assigned code or document tasks and inspect resulting local artifacts using model-selected actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: novel requirements, repository contents, command results, implementation errors, changed code and review feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the configured agent model selects its next coding/research actions and subsequent corrections within the dispatched task; SoduWork&#39;s optional Harness² text adapter also uses models to propose task-method changes before actual work. - Decision owner: configured external agent runtime for the substantive execution, not Hub&#39;s deterministic scheduler; SoduWork owns the packaged dispatch/receipt/rework integration, but does not claim the third-party agent&#39;s inner loop as original code. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: aiexec.dispatchsync, Runner adapter, task-specific isolated worktree, persisted workflow/outbox, fixed artifact receipts, task context, retry caps and execution policy. - Closure path: Hub task/approved plugin AI node → configured Runner invokes model-driven agent → agent observes repository/tools and performs work → versioned commit or file receipt returns to Hub → review feedback can dispatch a further agent work attempt. - Boundary reachability: README&#39;s supported Runner/agent integrations and bundled starter implement node connect this operating path to the ordinary self-hosted distribution; it is not merely an unshipped extension point. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model judgment controls execution contents and rework; the Hub&#39;s graph, permissions and delivery checks constrain and record these decisions but do not author them. S1 ownership is at the composite supported deployment boundary, not a claim of first-party model-loop implementation. - Evidence: starter plan and implementation, AI dispatch and pinned workspace, external execution adapters, text-only probe. - Basis: explicit composition and code reachability. - Confidence: high in dispatch and closure wiring; medium in generality across installed external agent kinds. - Caveats: no live paid-provider or full deployment test; a Hub installation with zero configured agents has no executing S1, and the external runtime implements inner decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Distinct S1 units: separate concurrently dispatched agent task executions, each owning its local model-directed implementation work on a registered Runner. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two or more independent task executions compete for bounded Runner capacity; a new dispatch can receive RUNNERATCAPACITY while another S1 holds execution slots. - Attenuating coordination relation: Hub&#39;s Runner-capability and tag routing, explicit capacity-busy classification and persisted outbox retry defer the displaced task instead of falsely marking it executed or corrupting the incumbent. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: after backoff and renewed admission the displaced S1 is actually started, processes the original task context and returns a real receipt; failure/busy is visible to the orchestration state meanwhile. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the source handles a named, observable interference among distinct operational task units over a finite common execution resource, and uses the returned capacity signal to alter when the next unit may act. - Function: attenuate runner-execution interference between separate agent tasks competing for bounded Runner capacity, allowing later S1 work to proceed without pretending a rejected dispatch succeeded. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous AI nodes requiring the same finite Runner slots, RUNNERATCAPACITY, unavailable/incompatible machines and delayed remote acknowledgements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party constructor-defined capacity routing, busy classification and scheduled requeue; there is no demonstrated model-owned arbitration of shared capacity. - Decision owner: SoduWork&#39;s deterministic Hub scheduler and agent-route selection, as constructed by the developer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Runner pick/capability scope and capacity result, RunnerCapacityBusy, requeuecapacitywait, persisted outbox claims and retry timing. - Closure path: multiple active AI node dispatches → a Runner reports capacity busy → Hub classifies rather than fails task work → moves waiting dispatch back to eligible queue → later capacity permits agent operation and receipt collection. - Boundary reachability: production workflowengine.executeai handles RUNNERATCAPACITY; the normal outbox tick path invokes capacity retry handling for registered Runner deployments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: this specific cross-task resource conflict and recovery path is packaged and operational, but its conflict handling is hard-coded rather than agent-decided, hence C rather than A. - Evidence: Runner busy classification and dispatch, Runner selection and execution, outbox recovery. - Basis: structural constructive, with a concrete capacity-collision witness. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this is narrow execution-capacity coordination; authored DAG ordering or agent-to-reviewer handoff alone would not suffice for S2.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Whole-system current view: the Hub&#39;s multi-task issue/assignment/status board and live workflow state expose ongoing team commitments, failed work and available owner routes across issues rather than just one agent turn. - Current-control decision scope: a legitimate human team operator can reassign current work, change priority and decide which stopped/ready issue to resume within the workspace&#39;s common operational capacity; the updates return through task state and assignment notifications. - Function: maintain team-wide current issue commitments, ownership and priority, and return human portfolio intervention to the working operational cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple simultaneous team requests, unassigned work, failed or blocked workflows, changing priorities and responsibilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a human team operator edits issue priority/assignment or chooses the next start/rework; Hub enforces authorization and pushes the changed assignment into the relevant task/agent dispatch path. - Decision owner: legitimate workspace operator/user in the shipped first-party parent-managed mode, not an autonomous S3 model. Automated scheduling and status transitions implement but do not own the portfolio judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: multi-task issue views, tasks update API with priority and assigneeUserId, task arrival notifications, workflow failure state, board status and user approval gates. - Closure path: Hub issue list/current workflow state exposes portfolio → operator revises priority, ownership or authorization across team issues → tasks persists change and emits assignment events → later task dispatch/owner view follows revised commitments. - Boundary reachability: the Hub/board and task mutation APIs ship with the standard multi-user application; the operator can close this parent loop without editing source or installing an extra plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: S3 is parent-owned human current control (P), while executor models remain bounded S1 agents. A human approval on one artifact alone is not the asserted S3 witness; the cross-issue state/commitment path is. - Evidence: task fields and reassignment, task and workflow state, team workspace UX. - Basis: parent-governed operating mode, structurally traced. - Confidence: medium; project/workspace permissions and actual operator behavior vary by deployment. - Caveats: no autonomous whole-current controller is established, and task-list visibility alone without reassignment/priority return would not qualify.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: an implementation agent&#39;s delivered commit is correct, secure, maintainable and satisfies the task&#39;s acceptance criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: the implementer returns a git revision/receipt and its own status through the Runner to the workflow engine; that result is not treated as sufficient audit evidence. - Complementary access path: the independent aireview node is dispatched with the immutable commit and a separate isolated worktree, and instructed to read that repository reality directly and produce a fresh code-review.json rather than reuse an upstream completion summary. - Independence boundary: a distinct review-node attempt with its own workspace, code read rights, pinned target, read-only/no-merge instruction and fresh structured findings; runtime configuration may still use the same vendor/model, so the claim is operational independence of evidence and action, not guaranteed model diversity. - Who acts on findings: workflowengine validates the review report, records attestation on approval or fails the node and returns change requests to implementation rework, subject to bounded retry/acceptance gates. - Function: independently examine an implementation&#39;s fixed code artifact against task acceptance and code quality requirements, and issue corrective findings to current execution control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect, insecure or unmaintainable implementations and false completion claims from the original executing agent. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separately dispatched reviewer agent reads the pinned code revision, forms an AI-authored structured verdict and findings; the Hub validates evidence shape and deterministically initiates rework on changesrequested. - Decision owner: independent review-node model for the substantive audit judgment; Hub owns immutable target enforcement, report validation, attestation and rework routing. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bundled aireview node separate from implement, per-node isolated worktree, fixed codeReviewTarget commit, no-write reviewer instruction, fresh code-review.json, workflowinputs.codereviewreport, and rejection/rework machinery. - Closure path: implementation commit receipt → reviewer dispatched to inspect fixed commit directly → code-review.json with verdict/findings → Hub refuses success on changes requested and returns findings to new implementation attempt → revised commit can be independently checked again. - Boundary reachability: the default starter plugin includes mandatory independent AI review before human acceptance; workflowengine supplies pinned-version review and processes its structured result in the normal live node polling path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a distinct model makes the fact-finding and correctness judgment with complementary access to the artifact itself, rather than trusting the implementation agent&#39;s status summary. Deterministic JSON checks and retry routing are enforcement, not the audit decision. - Evidence: shipped independent review stage, review isolation and locked target, review verdict/attestation processing. - Basis: explicit agent audit and corrective closure. - Confidence: medium-high for isolated audit path; medium for strong reviewer-model/provider independence in every installation. - Caveats: different node identity and isolated checkout do not guarantee different underlying foundation model; a review plugin that skips direct artifact examination would not satisfy this state in a particular run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no product-owned loop is established that prospectively scans environmental futures, selects adaptation options and returns changes to the team&#39;s future operating capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new plugin versions, agent providers, requested future work and changing task methods can arise, but these are administered or triggered per task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operators install or approve plugins, choose agents and methods; the optional Harness² adapter refines one immediate task plan, not future system adaptation policy. - Decision owner: human plugin/operator configuration and task-level model planning; no separate S4 authority is evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plugin catalog, version review, Harness² planning probe, task briefs and model-agent routing. - Closure path: changed task prompts or manually installed capability can reach next execution; no environmental forecast → option portfolio → prospective capability decision → operating return is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-method improvement (including text-only Harness² probes) does not by itself change the team&#39;s future-facing viable capability. - Evidence: Harness² text refinement, plugin governance. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a custom future-intelligence plugin could instantiate S4 beyond the reviewed packaged boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no completed identity or ultimate-policy decision concerning the team as a viable whole is shown within the shipped operational boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsafe plugin capabilities, delivery permissions, worker access, approval rights and artifact release are constrained by policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: humans grant access, approve individual deliveries and authorize plugin versions; static rule checks reject prohibited operations but do not adjudicate an identity/ultimate-policy conflict. - Decision owner: users and configured enforcement for ordinary authorization; no evidenced S5 matter is escalated to a legitimate ultimate-policy holder and returned as governing identity policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plugin governance queues, release-control approvals, role/permission checks, audit trail and runner admission. - Closure path: approval allows or blocks a plugin/task/release; there is no demonstrated organization-identity conflict → S5 judgment → changed governing mandate propagated to S3/S1. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: source-backed approval gates and access controls are real but not sufficient proof of S5 decision ownership. - Evidence: plugin governance, approval-driven task acceptance, workflow approval gates. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an organization may use these controls to implement external S5 governance, but that parent arrangement is not established by this source alone.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/23512ba54ae8461def5469ae68f5313aff017461/assessments/so/soduwork.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 23512ba54ae8461def5469ae68f5313aff017461\">23512ba &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T17:39:28+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/7ar-chat/harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">7ar harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/7ar-chat/harness/tree/89edbffde74f4aa2c7675dc07615f26ab76cae91\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 89edbffde74f4aa2c7675dc07615f26ab76cae91\">89edbff &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: a model-directed run selects and uses tools to produce the requested task outcome in a sandbox/host session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: diverse coding/information requests, filesystem and tool observations, failures, model responses and approval boundaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select tool calls, interpret results, continue/replan, produce task response or a code change/PR. - Decision owner: the configured live LLM-backed agent in each session or factory task; delegated agents own their bounded subtask choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ai/agent.Loop, sandbox adapters, tool registry, steering, durable events, approvals and per-task policy/budget hooks. - Closure path: model action → sandbox/tool result and event → next model turn or task conclusion; factory end hook records state, may request follow-up PR work. - Boundary reachability: the CLI and embedded/web hosts compose the loop; FactoryFeature installs a spawner and task dispatcher in the supported FACTORY=1 mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses the task work; queueing, policies, tool execution and approvals constrain but do not select the operational response. - Evidence: README quick start, single loop architecture, factory spawner and runtime hook. - Basis: structural; source paths and scripted tests, no live external model result claimed. - Confidence: high for first-party ownership/wiring. - Caveats: local backend does not itself isolate the host; a model key and applicable runtime configuration are required for real inference.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate conflicts among distinct task agents that share repository integration, dependencies and constrained execution capacity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing coding sessions/branches may conflict with the shared base, dependent tasks may start before prerequisites, and concurrent jobs may contend for quotas. - Decisive decision or feedback right: gate dependent task release, detect CI/merge conflicts, return actionable rebase/test feedback and defer incompatible integration; a whole-inter-S1 autonomous coordinator is not established. - Decision owner: deterministic factory task/forge mechanisms for admission and conflict detection; the receiving task agent decides its own fix, while the human/task policy controls merge permission. A function-specific constructor path exists, not a demonstrated autonomous S2 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: conditional task-state transitions, DependsOn release, MaxConcurrent, per-PR Mergeable/CI checks and MaxFixRounds feedback loop. - Closure path: dependency completion releases the waiting S1; an integration conflict or failing CI leaves the PR unmerged and passes feedback to the producing agent, which can rebase/fix and publish a new head for re-evaluation. - Boundary reachability: FactoryFeature composes tasks.Service and forge.Loop; these are invoked by the supported factory mode, not generic interfaces alone. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a repo-level S1 interaction is specifically regulated, but the decisions to defer/settle are mechanical or owner-governed; assigning model discretion to a named coordinator would overread the code. - Distinct S1 units: separate factory task agent sessions pursuing changes against one project repository, including bounded delegated sessions. - Inter-S1 disturbance: changes originating in different cells can invalidate a common base or require an ordering relation, and unchecked parallel execution can exceed current shared limits. - Attenuating coordination relation: dependency blocking/release and conflict-aware PR integration with CI/result/merge-state feedback, rather than unguarded simultaneous publication. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: ciFailed or conflictFeedback tells the same task agent to inspect, rebase/fix, test and reopen the PR; stored task state and checks are consulted before the next release or merge. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the witness concerns actual cross-cell interference at shared repository integration and prerequisite constraints; report collection, a tool queue or generic delegatemany alone is not being counted as S2. - Evidence: dependency state and release, CI and conflict feedback/resume, delegation isolation/limits. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium; inter-S1 interference/closure is explicit, agent-owned system-wide coordination is not. - Caveats: the factory documents fake-host end-to-end coverage, not verified production-scale contention resolution.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: whole-current oversight and interventions over an installed user&#39;s/project&#39;s agent sessions in supported team mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: blocked approvals, stuck/failing sessions, priority of attention and conflicting active work commitments visible across sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which session to continue, steer, approve/deny, cancel or start, after an all-visible-session view. - Decision owner: the legitimate authenticated user/project member authorizes risky acts; the supervisor agent observes and recommends but does not independently execute these acts without user confirmation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listsessions, readsession, watcher, supervised session tools, tool approval gate, membership access checks, audit and event feed. - Closure path: user examines the supervisor&#39;s system-wide status/readout → authorizes its concrete action → sessionsend/approve/cancel/spawn changes an ongoing run, its permission hold or the current work inventory; audit records the decision. - Boundary reachability: harness-web -team installs the first-party app/product Supervisor; a watcher can proactively surface exceptions, and the user can converse with the supervisor in its dedicated thread. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-based attention triage is present, but every material intervention tool is explicitly registered as risky and requires the human&#39;s confirmation in this mode; runtime budgets/dispatch alone are not S3 authority. - Whole-system current view: overviewFor and listSessions aggregate accessible personal/project sessions with status, project, creator, current wait and update time, augmented by readsession context. - Current-control decision scope: human-approved correction/termination, unblock and allocation of new session work across the user&#39;s visible installed operation; this is not a claim over unrelated tenant organizations. - Evidence: supervisor contract and risky approvals, whole-current list and acting tools, team settings. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the explicitly enabled human-owned mode. - Caveats: FACTORY=1 and -team are opt-in; a standalone one-session CLI has no asserted S3 loop. Factory queue/scheduler is not counted as autonomous S3.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the coding agent&#39;s completion claim before integration, using a direct code-host diff and a configured different reviewer model. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a coding agent may submit defective, insecure or task-incomplete changes while its own summary or CI gives an incomplete account. - Decisive decision or feedback right: emit approve/reject and findings on the exact PR; block merge or return fix feedback to the producing task. - Decision owner: a separately configured FACTORYREVIEWMODEL with explicit different-model enforcement for that task; mechanical security/test checks are supporting guards. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: forge direct PR diff/comments, mechanical protected-path/secrets/injection checks, CI-success requirement, model review rubric, head-specific recorded verdict and guarded merge. - Closure path: CI-ready PR → independent diff/reviewer judgment → forge.Loop.gate posts a verdict → approval allows guarded merge (only if AutoMerge) or findings resume/block the authoring S1, affecting the next attempt. - Boundary reachability: FactoryFeature installs the review gate when Forge is configured and review is not disabled; setting a distinct ReviewModel explicitly requires reviewer/author model inequality. This is a supported installed mode, not a borrowed external organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the configured reviewer model judges a code-host artifact without relying on the producing agent&#39;s report; default fallback to the same model is explicitly weaker and not credited as independent. - Claim being audited: the authoring factory agent&#39;s PR actually satisfies its task and is safe to integrate. - Ordinary reporting path: the author/agent task result and PR/CI status used by the normal factory work loop. - Complementary access path: the gate fetches actual PR patch/comments from GitHub, runs independent static checks and asks a distinct model to inspect the diff. - Independence boundary: distinct configured model checked by RequireDifferentModel, outside the producer&#39;s agent conversation; only this mode is counted, not the default same-model fallback. - Who acts on findings: factory PR loop withholds merging, publishes feedback and restarts the authoring task, or blocks for a human; successful verdict remains SHA-specific. - Evidence: reviewer distinct-model and direct-diff checks, gate feedback and guarded merge, factory feature review wiring, documented same-model caveat and setup limitation. - Basis: structural, conditional on the documented distinct-reviewer mode. - Confidence: medium; strongly wired source path, no actual independent live provider review was executed for this assessment. - Caveats: NoReview disables the gate; without a separate reviewer model the default self-review explicitly warns that it is not independent. Project CI and GitHub are complementary evidence sources, not ownership of the audit judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated installed decision loop that interprets external future change and selects/returns a prospective capability adaptation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new future tasks, repo practices, provider conditions and failures can require changed capabilities; present modes primarily address active or past tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S4-specific prospective adaptation judgment with a returned change in the installed operating model was found. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: optional factory lessons distillation and recall, memory, project configuration, cron triggers, coding benchmarks and feature extensibility. - Closure path: failed/completed task lessons can be recalled in later prompts; this changes operational context but does not select an external-and-future option and install a revised organizational capability under S4 control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-generated lessons and new task prompts are retrospective operating memory, not a demonstrated strategic environmental scan/adaptation closure. - Evidence: lessons feature, distillation/recall, docs factory/bench boundary. - Basis: bounded negative-state structural review. - Confidence: medium-high for shipped mode. - Caveats: an external project owner can redesign/deploy the system; contributor development is outside this deployed recursion and does not imply a shipped S4 parent loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: a legitimate human project owner settles and changes the installed project&#39;s identity, membership authority and binding operating boundaries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project/repository identity changes, member access conflicts and the limits under which assistant or factory agents may act. - Decisive decision or feedback right: create/update the project, change its repository/default branch and assign/revoke member roles; maintain at least one owner. - Decision owner: authenticated human project owner (and separately system admin for account approval), never the task agent or deterministic role checker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: owner-only routes, persistent Project/Member store, role-based guards on API/supervisor operations, audit/feed, per-task policy and merge-permission enforcement. - Closure path: identity/membership/policy issue → project owner edits authoritative project/roles (or approves a new actor) → changes persist and are enforced on subsequent session access, supervisor acts, factory task/project use and integration permissions. - Boundary reachability: harness-web -team provides owner-gated project patch/member routes; membership changes immediately govern user and agent-action access in the same installed product. No upstream maintainer authority is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent may propose work and act with human approval, but ultimate project membership, repository identity and operating authority remain owner-governed; static policy checks enforce their decisions. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: which repository constitutes the project and who may authorize/perform work on behalf of its organization. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: the project owner in team mode; no autonomous ultimate-policy mode established for a single CLI agent. - Return-to-operation path: owner-only PATCH /projects/{pid} or role assignment → stored project/role update → subsequent requireProject and server/agent tool checks recognize the new authority and branch/repo context. - Evidence: owner-only project/role changes, team membership and access model, factory policy/merge modes. - Basis: structural parent-governed team mode. - Confidence: medium; explicit authorization/return path, not a claim about the upstream OSS organization&#39;s policy process. - Caveats: ordinary tool approval or a single PR merge veto alone would not justify S5; the claimed right is the project-identity/member-authority loop.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8278b99a5810ef26bcf4000cc6213cb41cbc4149/assessments/7a/7ar-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8278b99a5810ef26bcf4000cc6213cb41cbc4149\">8278b99 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:46:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/stoicdataguy/asktravel-agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AskTravel Agent Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/stoicdataguy/asktravel-agent-harness/tree/9204095f29720ba70cdd90c9ccd6d214cda36c72\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9204095f29720ba70cdd90c9ccd6d214cda36c72\">9204095 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce researched trip advice and structured itineraries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: destinations, preferences, incomplete data, live tool observations and invalid itinerary arguments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose searches/playbooks, interpret observations and select/revise the itinerary. - Decision owner: the instantiated travelagent model actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LangChain execution, first-party tool wrappers, Postgres, retry/compaction middleware and Pydantic validation. - Closure path: tool and validation observations return to the model; presentitinerary persists and displays the chosen plan. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves storage/search wrappers without discretionary travel synthesis. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/graph.py; src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/api/openaicompat.py; src/travelagent/tools/itinerary.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Bounded source assessment of the supported distribution; no live model or performance claim. - Boundary reachability: API lifespan constructs buildagent; the public chat router invokes that graph with user context.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No supplied regulation of a specific inter-S1 disturbance is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel tools, independent chats and background upkeep must not be equated with coordinated autonomous work cells. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: thread/checkpoint branching, user namespaces, parallel tool execution and idle scheduling. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/api/threadsync.py; src/travelagent/agent/graph.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; src/travelagent/tools/memory.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-service current resource/commitment decision loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider rate limits and background workload are handled as configured runtime constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: retry backoff, capped users per maintenance cycle, API state and database persistence. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/retry.py; src/travelagent/config.py; src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge the travel actor&#39;s persisted user-fact claims against raw user statements outside the ordinary chat-production path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: misfiled interests/dislikes, contradicted or retracted saved facts and missing user-stated preferences. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge whether a saved user claim needs add/update/delete/noop and propose the smallest source-supported correction. - Decision owner: the separately prompted structured memory-review model invoked by llmproposer; not applyplan&#39;s deterministic matcher. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: idle-chat discovery, checkpoint access, guarded store writes, source-quote checks, sensitive-fact exclusion, MAXOPS and memorylog. - Closure path: the reviewer proposes corrections; accepted operations update the shared fact store, and graph.systemprompt reloads that store on subsequent model calls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the reviewer the scheduler and quote checker cannot decide semantic reclassification or contradiction; they only constrain/apply its proposed judgment. - Evidence: src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/config.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; src/travelagent/tools/memory.py; src/travelagent/agent/graph.py; tests/testconsolidation.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: A narrowly scoped source-supported memory-claim audit, not travel-answer verification. Same provider is permitted; trust is limited by selected recent messages, saved-fact evidence, model error, quote matching and excluded sensitive fields. No measured audit effectiveness. - Claim being audited: persisted user facts written by ordinary savememory calls faithfully represent user preferences/statements. - Ordinary reporting path: the travel actor writes summarized facts through savememory and later consumes their prompt projection. - Complementary access path: consolidateuser reads HumanMessage content directly from checkpoints and compares it to the store in a fresh structured review context. - Independence boundary: the producing chat actor neither selects the idle review invocation nor supplies its ordinary narrative as sole evidence; separate reviewer prompt and raw user messages can challenge that actor&#39;s stored claim. Shared provider/store do not establish global independence or coverage. - Who acts on findings: guarded applyplan updates memory and logs before/after; subsequent travel calls reread corrected facts. - Boundary reachability: app.lifespan creates the nonstreaming proposer and runconsolidationloop when default memoryconsolidation is enabled; disabled mode intentionally has no audit run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective environment-to-capability adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: live travel availability and changed user preferences are current-task/domain data. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: search tools, skill playbooks, memory consolidation and dynamic prompt refresh. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/prompts.py; src/travelagent/skills/init.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; docs/PLAN.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime ultimate-policy or identity decision-and-return path is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task preferences and data controls are not identity-level organizational disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system guidelines, sensitive-memory guard, settings and user export/delete endpoints. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/travelagent/agent/prompts.py; src/travelagent/config.py; src/travelagent/api/app.py; src/travelagent/consolidation.py; README.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5ae1eb582c8453ba6a02b1827749d624be95ba28/assessments/as/asktravel-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5ae1eb582c8453ba6a02b1827749d624be95ba28\">5ae1eb5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"3\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T16:44:57+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/eust-w/opengeniemaster\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenGenieMaster</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>3</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/eust-w/opengeniemaster/tree/8f6dab22a54a318bf8afa221269a6f9c00fa860c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8f6dab22a54a318bf8afa221269a6f9c00fa860c\">8f6dab2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: primary embodied domain work (e.g. inspect/restock, retrieve, clean, handoff) by agent-guided robot work cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user task variation, unavailable/unknown objects, visual occlusion, robot capability limits, unexpected failures, interruptions and contradictory task outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the in-process Claude coding agent chooses task decomposition, relevant tools, guidance, execution/recovery/handoff and whether to retry, based on live task context and returned observations. - Decision owner: ClaudeAgent in the optional first-party ogm agent mode; individual robot policy owns lower-level execution, not the higher-level coding-agent choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolRegistry, EEP schema, Harness, domain templates, adapters, admission interlocks, task store, completion criteria, typed failure/recovery hints, bounded turn count. - Closure path: goal → model tool decision → Harness.execute/robot → observation, checked outcome and persisted continuity → next model turn or terminal response. - Boundary reachability: documented ogm agent command constructs ClaudeAgent with first-party operator prompt and directly wired EEP tools; optional Anthropic SDK/API availability is an execution prerequisite. It is not necessary to invent an agent in application code. Offline fake-client tests demonstrate tool-loop wiring, not actual paid-model behaviour. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing ClaudeAgent leaves ScriptedOrchestrator, which can follow fixed recovery rules but does not retain the agent&#39;s open-ended choice of guidance and recovery under novel user contexts. S1 A belongs narrowly to the shipped optional Claude mode; the deterministic demo does not itself establish A. - Evidence: Claude tool loop, operator instructions, EEP handlers, execution/feedback, CLI agent entrypoint. - Basis: first-party optional agent tool loop with consequential environment feedback. - Confidence: high for code-level reachability, moderate for deployed agent outcomes (no provider E2E run). - Caveats: robot simulator is semantic; physical policy autonomy and real-world safety are not verified; provider model behaviour was not exercised.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: dampen conflicts between distinct embodied S1 work cells over capacity, shared work-item affinity and interrupted handoffs. - Distinct S1 units: two or more separately assigned robot/policy work cells with their own capability and physical state in a fleet; not two names for the same Claude agent. - Inter-S1 disturbance: assigning one busy robot simultaneously, transferring an unfinished pick–carry–place group while the first robot still holds its object, running dependent steps before prerequisites, or handing off to an incapable robot. - Attenuating coordination relation: Harness.dispatch excludes busy/stopped units, respects group affinity and dependencies; updatetask(assign/handoff) mediates exceptional reassignment. The agent&#39;s operating prompt directs failure-driven handoff and deliberate retry/wait/stop decisions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: assignment and handoff update persistent TaskStore; the next eeptaskget / eepdispatch / execution uses the new robot, group and dependency state. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the code explicitly detects the inter-robot work-item/capacity conflict and blocks/moves conflicting assignments, including held-object restart semantics; a simple shared task list would not do so. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent incompatible robot assignments and unstable cross-robot handoff in shared work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the autonomous Claude agent can choose to wait, hand off/reassign, force-restart affected work items, or route around failures based on robot feedback; deterministic dispatch enforces ordinary constraints but does not itself own discretionary S2 judgment. - Decision owner: ClaudeAgent for exception coordination in the supported multi-robot mode; deterministic Harness for bound-preserving enforcement. Routine default dispatch alone would support only constructor/enforcement, not A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: busy-robot set, capability filtering, group binding, held-object tracking, dependency graph, continuity persistence, readiness checks. - Closure path: two robot work cells create assignment/holding conflict → feedback/continuity visible through EEP → agent chooses handoff/retry/alternate robot → guard updates whole work item → subsequent dispatch/execution observes new ownership. - Boundary reachability: multi-robot scenarios and eeptaskupdate handoff are first-party tools available directly to ClaudeAgent; no custom coordinator is required in that mode. S2 A does not claim the scripted dispatcher has autonomous organizational discretion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the full exception-response choice can be made by the tool-using model; deterministic locks/guards are supporting regulators. Removing the model would retain fixed handoff mechanics but not its context-sensitive coordination discretion. - Evidence: operating prompt, dispatch and busy rules, handoff safeguards, continuity group assignment, handoff regression. - Basis: identifiable multi-S1 interference, conflict-specific guard, model-accessible discretionary recovery and returned assignment state. - Confidence: moderate: source and synthetic test wiring are clear; real multi-robot model behavior is untested. - Caveats: if physical robots are only passive actuators under one central actor, the organizational S2 interpretation narrows; standard simulated policy work cells still retain local execution environments and separate assignments.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: provide a current whole-fleet task/priority/resource regulation path, beyond local per-step delegation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous active tasks competing for finite robot availability, differing priorities, stopped robots and unmet commitments. - Whole-system current view: eeptasklist lists all tasks, eepcapabilities lists fleet skills/status, Harness.busyrobots spans active, paused and interrupted tasks, and ScriptedOrchestrator.runready enumerates all active tasks. - Current-control decision scope: assign resources across all ready tasks, use task priority when selecting the next work item, stop/pause or restart commitments and intervene on assignment. Task priority is chosen at creation, not autonomously renegotiated by a dedicated whole-system governor. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a developer/composed operator or future first-party agent policy must decide whole-system priorities, commitments and exceptions; the shipped scheduler executes preselected priorities and capacity rules. - Decision owner: no autonomous agent demonstrably assigned sustained whole-system S3 discretion in the standard operating instructions. The caller chooses priorities and when the scheduler runs; scripts enforce them. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: priority-sorted task store, cross-task dispatch, runready highest-priority selection, stop/resume and event log. - Closure path: all-task view + priorities → scheduled fleet assignment/intervention → persistent task/robot state → next scheduling pass. The first-party constructor is real; an autonomous whole-system deciding/feedback actor must still be composed. - Boundary reachability: eeptasklist, priority at eeptaskcreate, cross-task eepdispatch and ScriptedOrchestrator.runready are shipped callable modes. No private benchmark oracle is needed. This is a function-specific S3 path, not mere generic tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: scripted runready orders work by caller-supplied priority; Claude&#39;s documented ogm agent goal prompt serves one task and does not establish an autonomous sustained fleet-wide current-control review. Enforcement is not ownership. Human eeptaskupdate calls are local operations, not a distinct operationally closed parent S3 mode. - Evidence: all-task and fleet view, task priorities and dispatch, cross-task busy guard, scheduler runready, tool registry. - Basis: shipped S3-specific whole-current-task capacity/priority constructor with missing autonomous decisive S3 policy. - Confidence: moderate-high. - Caveats: no operator-governed (P) whole-system governance closure, budget bargaining, or accountable strategic priorities is demonstrated.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: challenge the robot policy&#39;s own success report and the ordinary step-completion record through a separately callable, more direct evidence check. - Claim being audited: the physical action succeeded and its effect persists (e.g. an item really remains in the intended slot, not just policy-reported success). - Ordinary reporting path: robot adapter execute returns a policy success/outcome and observation; harness evaluates immediate criteria, records execution and updates task status. - Complementary access path: eepverify / recheckstep later calls criterion checks, optionally at close range, through the adapter&#39;s separate check operation; contradictory results mark prior execution records, convert succeeded steps to failed, and reopen completed tasks. - Independence boundary: the verification request and criterion signal are separate from the policy&#39;s initial self-reported success, and can overturn it in the shipped simulator. However physical HttpAdapter.check calls the same configured robot service (/check), with no guaranteed independent detector/operator/credential ownership; the model can also invoke the check itself. This is an explicit complementary constructor, not a demonstrated organizationally independent S3 auditor. - Who acts on findings: the harness reconciles continuity deterministically, and the coding agent can read discrepancies, recover or create new memory; a distinct audit owner/trigger/sampling authority is not supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false-positive policy completion, persistent-effect regressions and false negatives hidden in routine execution summaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an independent auditor&#39;s selection of when/how to check and corrective judgment is not operationally assigned; the first-party alternative-evidence and contradiction-to-rework feedback path is implemented. - Decision owner: needs first-party-mode integration/developer composition for independent S3 authority; Harness.recheckstep owns deterministic reconciliation, not independent audit judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: criteria API, alternate camera/close-range flag, contradiction records, task reopening and offline regression tests. - Closure path: policy claim → later direct criterion check → contradiction/reopening and marked evidence → S1 rework and future reflection. Distinct auditor authority is the missing component for A. - Boundary reachability: eepverify is exposed to the built-in Claude and MCP agents and Harness.recheckstep is shipped in the runtime; this is more than a test-only audit, though real detector independence is not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a same-loop verifier can provide valuable extra evidence but does not prove a separately controlled independent audit; C recognizes the material constructor without fabricating A. - Evidence: recheck and task reopening, robot adapter direct check, tool eepverify, regression showing overturned completion, criteria. - Basis: separate criterion and corrective-return path, conditional independence at the evidence level, missing independent audit ownership for A. - Confidence: moderate; stronger simulation path than unverified real backend. - Caveats: same backend may correlate failures; normal immediate completion-criterion checks alone would not suffice for S3.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: expose a capability-adaptation path using observed environmental difficulty and prospective targeted rehearsal, then return learned alternative guidance/coordination into subsequent work. - External distinction: repeated physical-world failures in particular robot/model/skill/object contexts, e.g. occluded targets, look-alike stock, changed item placements and slow handling, compared across execution records and domain catalog. - Future / prospective distinction: capability-gap severity and planned idle-time practice for troublesome contexts before the next customer/work task; this is an opportunity to develop/rehearse future response capacity, not just retry the current failed step. - Adaptation option generated: capabilitygaps groups recent failures, relative attempts and slowness; planpractice generates low-priority repeated pick/return tasks; RuleReflector or optional LLMReflector mines successful recoveries and contradictions into contextual patch instructions. - Path back into current capability / S3: practice runs produce execution records → evidence-checked SharedAdmission admits or rejects guidance/recovery/criteria/coordination patches → GuidanceBuilder and chooserobot apply relevant memory on later dispatch/execution; weak non-safety memory can be retired based on outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: foreseeable performance deterioration or new capability demands in observed external object/robot contexts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: whether and when to run future-oriented practice, what capacity to spend, which gap to prioritize, and whether to make this a standing adaptation program. eepcapabilitygaps(schedulepractice=true) is callable, but no automatic idle/foresight owner and no sustained whole-system adaptation conversation with S3 are assigned in the standard runtime. - Decision owner: caller/developer must compose the autonomous S4 agenda and authority; deterministic gap ranking/practice creation and evidence-gated memory support do not by themselves own prospective choice. An optional Claude agent could call the constructor, but the shipped operator prompt lacks a recurring future-environment scan/idle planning loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: execution record history, contextual failure aggregation, low-priority practice workflow, rule/LLM reflection, schema/evidence/safety admission, versioned long-term memory and replay tests. - Closure path: environmental difficulty → exposed gap and practice option → caller-scheduled rehearsal → feedback/reflection/admitted patch → changed future guidance/robot preference. The function-specific construction path exists, but autonomous S4 initiative remains to be composed. - Boundary reachability: eepcapabilitygaps with schedulepractice and memory tools are actually in the shipped tool registry, not just a README roadmap; rule reflection and admitted patches run in standard simulation and the MCP mode. Physical extrapolation not claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a reflective memory loop alone is not S4. The distinct prospective practice option and future capability return make this more than generic memory, while lack of a scheduled autonomous environmental/adaptation governor keeps it at C. - Evidence: capability-gap/practice construction, tool exposure, reflection, admission safety/evidence, memory application, architecture memory path. - Basis: first-party prospective gap/practice-to-later-capability constructor; no complete autonomous S4 decision loop or parent mode. - Confidence: moderate. - Caveats: gaps are mined from internal execution records of externally situated work, not broad market/strategy sensing. There is no robot-weight self-training; flywheel.py stops at dataset export.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: would decide or revise ultimate identity/mission and balance current robot operations against the future adaptation program. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unresolved mission/safety/strategic-policy tensions beyond routine robot-task control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party runtime path accepts a genuinely ultimate-policy question, routes it to legitimate authority, records an authoritative identity decision and returns it to govern subsequent operation. - Decision owner: none evidenced for an active S5 loop; hard-coded safety constraints and user task goals do not constitute an identity governor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: domain safety rules, SharedAdmission rejection of unsafe guidance/memory, robot stop/resume, current-model prompt, human teleoperation example, repo contributor governance. - Closure path: not established at ultimate-policy level; operational stop/resume and evidence-based memory patches affect tasks or safety settings rather than deciding identity or arbitrating S3–S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent cannot revise ultimate mission under a dedicated first-party process, and no first-party parent institution/human S5 escalation-return path is supplied. Accepting task goals and protecting immutable safety flags are ordinary operating constraints. - Evidence: operator prompt/safety, admission rules, memory admission cannot weaken safety, robot stop/resume, human backend, research vs repo identity note. - Basis: bounded negative conclusion for reviewed runtime rather than universal impossibility. - Confidence: moderate-high within shipped modes. - Caveats: a real deployment may have human/organizational identity governance outside this repository, but that external authority cannot be inferred here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1c021299b02811b5e34a4b01833cdba494fca0b3/assessments/op/opengeniemaster.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1c021299b02811b5e34a4b01833cdba494fca0b3\">1c02129 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T14:37:34+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/uniac-ai/AgentOrg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentOrg</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/uniac-ai/AgentOrg/tree/25e116cb719989ce568b272016b1936668efbf5f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 25e116cb719989ce568b272016b1936668efbf5f\">25e116c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: attached autonomous agent sessions carry out locally variable project work and return artifacts/results as Work submissions within their declared position. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing project requirements, source/tool results, work feedback and manager requests that demand operational judgment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select task actions and revise work within the Actor&#39;s purpose, then submit a result and respond to returned review or ask answers. - Decision owner: attached Claude Code/Codex or other autonomous agent session in the documented first-party MCP operating mode; operator supplies initial position/credentials and task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Hub Actor registry, MCP adapter, Workspace records, audit store and session hooks, Provider provisioning (optional). - Closure path: agent receives assigned Work/attention, acts in its own project runtime, records progress and submits; requester reviews or changes assignment, after which the session receives and acts on returned state. - Boundary reachability: the distributed MCP adapter and bundled skill explicitly attach supported agent sessions and supply the Work/attention/submit path. This is a wired operating mode, not merely an open-ended API a developer might someday connect. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: cognition and project actions are in the attached external agent, credited as an actor only because the installed mode explicitly wires its decision/action/report loop; Hub persistence alone is not S1 autonomy. - Evidence: MCP agent client/attachment, Work bindings, agent Work protocol. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Hub alone creates no model session and cannot guarantee external agent behavior; no paid provider runtime was executed in this review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent simultaneous operational Actors from destructively claiming the same shared resource while they do distinct Work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two active S1 positions can contend for one release lane, cluster or other shared named resource. - Decisive decision or feedback right: caller seeks/renews/releases a resource claim; Hub refuses another active holder and names the conflict; an agent must compose its own response, negotiation and later retry. - Decision owner: the holder/contender Actor selects a requested resource and intended follow-up; deterministic Hub claims enforce exclusive holding and expiry, but the standard distribution does not install an autonomous conflict-resolution policy for all Actors. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SQLite claim store, lifetime/holder checks, authenticated Actor identity, conflict refusals and root override. - Closure path: the second claim returns a named-holder refusal; a custom agent policy or manager can subsequently wait, negotiate, change its work or retry after release/expiry, using the first-party claim path. - Boundary reachability: named claims are an explicit shipped Hub Tool available to attached Actors and the root, and the skill documents their shared-resource purpose. The autonomy/coordination response after refusal requires composition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: concrete exclusive-claim regulation is first-party and function-specific, but its mandatory refusal/expiry judgment is deterministic and no universal autonomous coordination-closure actor is installed. This warrants constructor C rather than A. - Distinct S1 units: separately attached active agent positions in one Hub organization, each with its own Work and local task environment. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing claims for the same named shared lane or resource. - Attenuating coordination relation: exclusive holder check with conflict identity and explicit release/expiry. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: refusal tells the contender who holds the resource; custom agent/operator behavior can adapt task choice or retry once the claim ends. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: claims.ts enforces a specific shared-resource collision and returns a conflict, rather than merely transferring messages or exposing untyped storage. - Evidence: claim implementation, domain Tool catalog, bundled skill organization commands. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: no mandatory agent-side claim discipline or automatic release notification/negotiation is established; the Hub&#39;s isolated conflict decision is not by itself evidence of agent S2 ownership.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current organization-wide or managerial-subtree commitments by assigning Work, responding to subordinate asks and accepting or returning submitted results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: reports may block, diverge from assigned work, need approvals or consume commitments that must be reviewed and reprioritized by a responsible manager. - Decisive decision or feedback right: see current direct-report Work/asks, choose ownership and goals, answer/escalate actionable asks, review a specific submission and return changes or accept it. - Decision owner: in the agent-manager mode, the attached manager&#39;s agent exercises this discretionary current-control right; in the distinct root CLI mode, the legitimate human root can make equivalent authoritative choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: team, actorview, Work and Forms records, role checks, attention/waiter delivery and stored revisions. - Closure path: manager observes report/ask/submission, chooses answer/reassignment or workreview verdict, Hub stores it, and the subordinate receives attention and updates/resubmits Work. Human root mode uses the same return path. - Boundary reachability: the standard-distribution agent skill directly instructs managers to inspect team state, assign/review subordinate Work and handle asks; the shipped root CLI can act as the canonical human root with the Hub&#39;s authenticated rights. Both modes use the same installed Work/attention path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in manager-agent mode, the agent selects which current intervention to make; deterministic record/authority checks enforce it. In root mode, the human is the decision owner. These are alternative modes, not concurrent owners of one decision. - Whole-system current view: manager&#39;s team and actorview show its reports&#39; Work state, current asks and review queue; canonical root can see the whole organization. - Current-control decision scope: assigning Work goals/owners, reviewing submitted commitments, answering/escalating blocking asks and changing subsequent report activity. - Evidence: team/attention projections, Workspace operation bindings, agent manager procedure, root CLI authority. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: not every connected Actor is a manager; an agent-specific actual review choice was not exercised with a provider. Human root control is specifically current-work supervision, not an S5 identity-policy claim.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no shipped independent complementary inspection of operational reality beyond ordinary work reporting and manager reviews is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workers can misreport completion, but a separate audit-specific counter-observation path is not provided by the Hub. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party independent raw-artifact audit judgment with corrective return is established. - Decision owner: managers review submissions through ordinary Work state; the root reads operation metadata, neither becomes a distinct packaged S3 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: append-only activity metadata, Work review verdicts, status/attention, Hub audit events and client context. - Closure path: review changes can return to a worker as S3 accountability, not an independently sourced complementary operational audit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a root activity read observes tool-call metadata, while workreview is normal reporting/review; no separately deployed direct-evidence auditor is wired. - Evidence: activity and Work boundaries, audit metadata code, manager skill. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a connected organization&#39;s external agents can run tests or review code, but that independently configured audit is not a default Hub function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective environment/option-generation and return-to-current-capability loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external demand and capability changes may affect the organization but are not converted by a shipped S4 process into adaptation choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct future-oriented model, option selection and S3 return path. - Decision owner: an agent/user may propose new work, but the Hub has no S4-specific owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Work backlog, reports/asks, status, provider catalog, generic messages and stored historical activity. - Closure path: no shipped external-prospecting option-to-implementation path; Work assignments remain present-task control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: storage and future-dated Work do not show externally grounded environmental modeling or adaptation. - Evidence: Tool catalog and projections, agent skill. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: organization-specific skills might implement prospective assessment; they are external to this reviewed standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: the Hub models organization membership/purpose and enforces role authority, but no first-party identity/ultimate-policy deliberation and authoritative return loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: creating/closing a position, credential revocation and action approvals are governed structural operations, not necessarily policy/identity tensions of the whole organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct workflow takes an ultimate-policy issue to legitimate authority, decides organizational ethos/policy and returns an enforceable new policy into operation. - Decision owner: human root chooses membership and routine configuration; agent managers can create authorized reports. Neither is evidenced as an S5-specific decision path merely from hierarchical titles or rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Actor registry, credentials, lifecycle checks, hierarchy, approval Forms and filtered interfaces. - Closure path: Actor create/close and approval answers change ordinary access/work; absent is an explicit identity-policy issue/decision/returned governance process. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a root actor and purpose fields do not themselves prove the VSM S5 function; generic Forms allow many applications but do not compose ultimate policy. - Evidence: registry/lifecycle, Hub design authority, agent skill approval limits. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a particular organization may implement genuine parent S5 over these generic tools, but that case requires its own first-party operating contract and evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/448a5f4f4273aa7aaa5175f4e71e491562962a2d/assessments/ag/agentorg.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 448a5f4f4273aa7aaa5175f4e71e491562962a2d\">448a5f4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-13T22:41:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ShuaiMXu/happyhands\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HappyHands</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-13\">2026-11-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ShuaiMXu/happyhands/tree/d469a02cb9b98c5f1a85b05201f5401bbec20ebf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d469a02cb9b98c5f1a85b05201f5401bbec20ebf\">d469a02 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Implement a scoped product-experience repair or page composition in the isolated target. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different UI defects, source conditions and verification evidence require task-specific implementation choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose how to reproduce a finding, whether it is actionable, and which bounded source edits resolve it. - Decision owner: The configured implementation agent reached through the shipped runner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Source-bound plan, selected finding set, worktree, execution limit, captured originals and structural checks. - Closure path: The Workbench creates or reads a confirmed scope, invokes the runner, captures the changed preview and records checks and the result for review. - Boundary reachability: The automatic repair action and the CLI execute command both reach the bundled isolated runner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the agent the harness can capture and validate, but it cannot make the supported task-specific implementation decisions. - Evidence: docs/harness.md, docs/harness.md, packages/workbench/service.js, packages/harness-core/runner.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Repair authority is user-delegated. Final QA and acceptance are separate decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Regulate interference among multiple independent S1 production units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent product edit work or shared component edits could interfere if the harness supplied multiple coequal workers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No material inter-S1 coordination right is shipped in the assessed product loop. - Decision owner: None established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Run identity, per-run action and stage locks, isolated worktrees and job capacity. - Closure path: The Workbench serializes incompatible stages in a single run and isolates separate executions. Review batches return QA evidence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The operating chain uses one implementation worker per repair. Reviewers and deterministic checks are different organizational roles, and the inspected job controls supply no decision path for interference among distinct S1 units. - Evidence: packages/workbench/service.js, packages/workbench/direction-execution.js, docs/architecture.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Regulate the active review and repair commitment from source-bound evidence and verification state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stale or false findings, insufficient evidence, failed repair and revised scope can invalidate the current commitment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Determine which scoped work may run, and on failed QA decide whether to reject or direct a fresh evidence-bound review. - Decision owner: The product operator using the Workbench or CLI. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Run-wide state, source binding, per-stage gates, explicit repair requests, a retained worktree and judgment records. - Closure path: The operator chooses a repair scope or a new evidence-backed review and the service binds that choice to the plan or review. Rejection ends that run and acceptance requires a separate QA pass. - Boundary reachability: Shipped Workbench actions and CLI tools expose source review, execution authorization and result control over the same ledger. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The implementation agent judges the edits inside its remit. The operator owns intervention on the current commitment and the disposition of the result. - Whole-system current view: For the assessed product run the ledger and workbench aggregate baseline, findings, plan, worktree result, after evidence, QA and judgment. - Current-control decision scope: Scope the active repair, reject the result or return it for evidence-bound review before the next decision. - Evidence: docs/harness.md, docs/harness.md, packages/workbench/service.js, packages/harness-core/workflow.js. - Basis: structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently audit whether the change achieves the user task without material visual regression. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The executor&#39;s report and deterministic layout checks can miss actual experience regressions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Issue a visual verdict from directly captured, source-bound before and after evidence. - Decision owner: The separate configured Visual QA model session in the shipped model-review mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Immutable originals, coverage and state binding, checks, separate review context and judgment gate. - Closure path: A failing or incomplete review blocks acceptance. New evidence can trigger a fresh review, whose result governs the next operator disposition. - Boundary reachability: The Workbench review path invokes the shipped reviewer after recapture and structural evaluation. The lightweight CLI also exposes model review. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The independent reviewer reasons over direct evidence and reports findings, while the runtime checks provenance and enforces gates. - Claim being audited: The repaired user experience meets the task without unacceptable visual regression. - Ordinary reporting path: Implementation result, changed files, executor claims and structural evaluation. - Complementary access path: Matched original browser captures and bound specialist evidence in a separate review session. - Independence boundary: Visual QA is produced separately from the implementation session and checks the actual changed render. - Who acts on findings: The workflow blocks acceptance and the product operator can reject or direct new evidence and independent re-review. - Evidence: docs/experience-orchestration.md, packages/workbench/ai-review.js, packages/workbench/ai-review.js, packages/harness-core/workflow.js. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Develop and return a prospective adaptation of the harness&#39;s future product-review capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changed product needs and repeated visual failures may motivate new method choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select a reviewed adaptation and make it affect future operation. - Decision owner: The operator can admit cases and approve durable design changes. A maintainer can publish and activate Expertise metadata. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Source-bound case admissions, future-plan retrieval, independent regression reports and versioned release pointers. - Closure path: Admitted cases can enter later plans as references. Expertise activation changes a release pointer, but the inspected operational runner did not establish that the pointer changes the loaded capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The concrete case path changes context for later work. The inspected release path has a gap between recording activation and changing operational method selection. - Evidence: docs/experience-cases.md, docs/project-memory-lifecycle-2026-10-18.md, packages/skill-core/releases.js, packages/skill-core/releases.js. - Basis: structural -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Set the durable design and experience rules that govern future product work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Observed or proposed visual conventions may conflict with the product&#39;s intended identity and standards. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide whether proposed brand, design and reusable rules are approved, replaced or deprecated as the authoritative Foundation. - Decision owner: The human product owner/operator authorized to approve the product&#39;s Foundation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Observed inventory, explicit reasoned decisions, version binding and plans constrained by the approved snapshot. - Closure path: The extracted or staged proposal reaches the operator, whose approval writes an immutable Foundation and updates the Experience Spec. Bootstrap plans and execution load and check the same approved version. - Boundary reachability: The shipped workbench and CLI expose Foundation approval, with a ledger gate and a subsequent runner check. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Extraction and generation implement or propose design choices. The human decides what visual language is authoritative for the product. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Which visual language, design principles, brand voice and reusable design rules future product work must preserve. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: The product operator acting for the product through explicit Foundation approval. - Return-to-operation path: The approved version becomes a prerequisite and input to first-render planning and execution. - Evidence: docs/architecture.md, docs/harness.md, packages/harness-core/store.js, packages/harness-core/runner.js. - Basis: structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/75bb11fb29027ed579e4d24016a815b3d95c710c/assessments/ha/happyhands.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 75bb11fb29027ed579e4d24016a815b3d95c710c\">75bb11f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T23:02:10+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sgeos/pydromon\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pydromon</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sgeos/pydromon/tree/65671672b812b1d4c88f8e7633fdd3a1e50bb5f0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 65671672b812b1d4c88f8e7633fdd3a1e50bb5f0\">6567167 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Configured session lines produce project work and exchange results through their first-party daemon composition. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User tasks, local workspace changes, messages, background runs and engine/tool failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose local tools, communications, work actions and whether to continue or end a turn. - Decision owner: The model-backed session-line actor; external Claude/Codex/Pi engines supply inference/tool-loop dependencies rather than autonomous Dromon metasystem judgments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party driver construction, tool exposure, mailbox/event log, wake scheduler, sandbox/settings composition and persisted engine sessions. - Closure path: Queued mail/wake → line prompt and configured engine → chosen tools/messages/work → recorded turn outcomes → later line turns. - Boundary reachability: The distributed daemon creates configured session lines with first-party Dromon tools and driver/engine wiring. The supported Claude/Codex-backed composition is executable without implementing a new harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the model-backed line actors, the daemon transports messages and enforces policy but cannot choose meaningful project work. Dromon does not receive credit for unrelated engine features. - Evidence: [Scheduler][R1], [engine composition][R2], [daemon][R3], [line configuration][R4], [README][R5]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: No live subscribed engine or paid model was run; engine independence and operational quality are not claimed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Declared resource leases and role-seat ownership regulate concrete shared-resource/write collisions among operational lines. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two active lines may try to use the same named project resource; role seats sharing a worktree must avoid competing writers and stale ownership transitions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A line chooses when to acquire/release a resource lease and can hand off or recover role ownership subject to observed eligibility. - Decision owner: Configured agent lines own invocation/release/handoff choices; the operator declares permitted resource names/role terms, and deterministic state validates exclusivity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Lease holder/expiry events, refusal with holder/expiration feedback, non-renewal/TTL, role owner invariants, engine access rebuilding and journaled handoff. - Closure path: Contender calls acquire → receives grant or current-holder/expiry refusal → adjusts later resource use; release/expiry enables another acquisition. Role handoff records a note, transfers authority, wakes the successor and rebuilds the former owner&#39;s access. - Boundary reachability: acquirelease/releaselease and seat ownership tools are supplied to running lines by Scheduler; project-config leases/roles enable the shipped path, not a custom conflict resolver. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The actors decide when to reserve/release/pass work; the log and guards enforce those decisions. This credits that bounded agent-mediated coordination mode, not generic messaging or the scheduler itself. - Evidence: [Lease tool and role tool implementations][R1], [role invariants/one-writer boundary][R6], [project enablement][R7], [line tool semantics][R4]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Resource leases are cooperative: unmanaged external processes or ignored lease conventions are not forcibly excluded. Deterministic automatic failover is support, not autonomous discretion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: The project operator regulates the whole current set of session lines, role capacity, budgets and continuing commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Runaway autonomous turns/cost, held or failed lines, exhausted provider plans, conflicting current staffing and engine availability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Review project-wide state and choose line/role/budget/hibernation/rebind/interruption/release changes or answer a held line. - Decision owner: The authenticated local project operator, acting as the legitimate parent of the project run. No first-party autonomous whole-project controller is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Private operator socket, all-line status summaries, durable events, operator-attributed messages, hold notices/question queue, CLI commands and scheduler/engine enforcement. - Closure path: Whole-project status or held-line notice → operator current-control choice → daemon command/event → scheduler applies limits, ownership, model, hold or interruption changes → subsequent turns run under the returned decision. - Boundary reachability: Daemon.execute serves operator-authority status/settings/role/budget/configure/release/interrupt commands, and status includes every line. The first-party scheduler routes hold questions and operator answers back into later operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the operator leaves fixed limits and failover machinery, not discretionary whole-system resource/commitment revision. P describes the supplied supervised mode; enforcement does not become A or C. - Evidence: [Operator protocol dispatch/current status][R3], [holds and operator-answer scheduler][R1], [project limits/roles][R7], [role runtime updates][R6], [client protocol][R8]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: The parent boundary is this local project, not the public OSS organization; operator socket access is an explicit trust assumption.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No installed complementary independent audit judgment-and-corrective-return loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect work-completion claims, stale notes, disclosure mistakes and engine-reporting errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separate audit-specific actor or constructor adjudicates operational truth and returns corrective control. - Decision owner: Lines report their work; runtime observes process/session/log facts. Read-only seats are configured by users without a supplied audit workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Daemon-stamped commit hashes, role journals, activity/event logs, raw transport evidence/replay and disclosure checks. - Closure path: Facts can be inspected by an operator or peer; the standard product has no audit verdict/corrective-action protocol for a particular completion claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Independent fact capture is useful evidence but is not itself discretionary complementary audit; tests, developer reviews and optional advisory seats do not supply an installed audit function. - Evidence: [Scheduler status/journal/check tools][R1], [engine transport evidence][R2], [evidence store][R9], [role design][R6], [line tools][R4]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: A user can organize reviewer lines through general messages; that application would need separate function/independence/closure evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider budget changes, new model choices and suggested project patches are available current inputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped intelligence actor develops future-oriented capability options and closes their return into current capability. - Decision owner: Operator chooses reconfiguration/upgrades; ordinary lines may submit untrusted patch proposals. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: proposechange storage, project reload/rebind, provider-budget failover and optional local teacher lifecycle. - Closure path: Proposals are explicitly never applied by Dromon; teacher code only loads/unloads a local model. Rebinding/failover responds to present availability rather than prospective adaptation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A future roadmap, a model called teacher, context handoff or a current capacity switch is not S4. Generic operator patch handling does not create a function-specific parent adaptation loop. - Evidence: [Proposal no-apply contract][R10], [teacher boundary][R11], [role failover][R6], [reload/rebind][R3]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Repository-development/dogfood decisions and future Rust/Keleusma ports are adjacent.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No function-specific identity/ultimate-policy decision-return loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Access/disclosure rules, operator messages, project instructions and role terms. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No identity-level issue is specifically adjudicated and returned by ultimate authority as a new governing policy. - Decision owner: Operator configures the project and its permission/disclosure boundaries; agents have explicitly bounded local tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Operator/line address attribution, private socket trust, disclosure checks, tool/recipient policies, protected state and role rules. - Closure path: Configuration and operator answers alter current execution, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue/decision protocol or S3–S4 balance closure is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The operator is a real parent for S3 current control; that does not promote all configuration or question replies to S5. - Evidence: [Operator and line authority split][R3], [line tools/policy][R4], [project configuration][R7], [scheduler trust and question semantics][R1]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: First-party development documents record human design decisions, but those are not an installed policy actor in an arbitrary user project.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b8dbf77260552d46200e82343dce1fc8cd20a3db/assessments/py/pydromon.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b8dbf77260552d46200e82343dce1fc8cd20a3db\">b8dbf77 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
